/* Academic Language · Academic English Unit 2 (e1u2) · Sentences and paragraphs */
ICA.addUnit({
  id:"e1u2", track:"e1", n:2, title:"Sentences and paragraphs", icon:"✍️",
  blurb:"The language of academic writing, sentence by sentence: formal and informal choices, sentence patterns that join ideas clearly, the grammar points that trouble most writers (articles, countable nouns, agreement, tense and the passive), paragraphs that hold together, and claims that are exactly as strong as the evidence, in as few words as clarity allows.",
  goals:[
    "Choose formal wording for an academic essay (full forms, precise verbs, specific words, no casual “you”) while knowing which phrasal verbs are perfectly normal",
    "Build simple, compound and complex sentences, join ideas with the right subordinator or transition, and fix run-ons, comma splices, fragments and faulty parallel structure",
    "Use articles, countable and uncountable nouns, subject–verb agreement, tense and the passive accurately in academic writing, and know where conventions vary",
    "Write paragraphs with a clear topic sentence, support and a closing or linking sentence, held together by given-to-new order, clear reference and accurate transition signals",
    "Hedge and boost claims with modal verbs, adverbs and phrases such as “appears to” and “it is likely that”, and cut empty words without cutting meaning"
  ],
  read:[
    {book:"owg", where:"Handbook, sections H2 “Paragraphs and Transitions”, H3 “Clear and Effective Sentences”, H4 “Sentence Errors”, H5 “Words and Language” and H7 “Verbs”", note:"A free handbook written for US college students. It covers most of this unit’s grammar with short examples; it does not cover articles or countable nouns, which this unit teaches in Lesson 3."},
    {book:"phrasebank", where:"General Functions: “Being cautious”, “Comparing and contrasting”, “Explaining causality” and “Signalling transition”", note:"Ready-made academic phrases, sorted by the job they do. Use them as models for your own sentences."}
  ],
  lessons:[
  /* ===================== LESSON 1 ===================== */
  {id:"e1u2-l1", title:"Formal and informal choices", minutes:50,
   hook:{q:"A classmate tells you: “Never use phrasal verbs in academic writing.” Which sentence shows that this advice is too simple?", options:["The team carried out interviews in four districts.","We didn’t get much data.","Lots of studies say this.","The results were really nice."], answer:0, reveal:"**“Carried out” is a phrasal verb, and it is completely normal in research articles.** So are “set up” and “point out”, and other multi-word verbs such as “focus on” and “rely on”. The real advice is narrower: some phrasal verbs, such as “look into” or “come up with”, sound conversational, and a single verb such as “investigate” or “devise” is often more precise. The other three sentences each have a different informal feature (a contraction, “lots of”, “really nice”). This lesson sorts out which choices matter and why."},
   goals:[
     "Explain what “formal” means in academic English, and why it is about precision more than long words",
     "Replace contractions, conversational phrasal verbs, the general “you” and vague words in a formal essay",
     "Recognise phrasal verbs that are standard in academic writing, and keep the grammar right when you swap in a single verb",
     "Choose US or UK spelling and use it consistently"
   ],
   learn:[
    {h:"What “formal” really means", body:[
      {lead:"Think of the difference between talking to a friend at lunch and explaining something to a room of people you don’t know. You still use the same language, but you choose your words more carefully, because your listeners can’t stop you to ask what you mean."},
      "Academic writing is like that second situation. Your reader may be a tutor, an examiner or a researcher on the other side of the world. They can’t ask you questions, so your words must carry the meaning on their own. That is the main reason academic English has a **formal** style. The usual name for this style is [[academic-register|academic register]] (Research Skills Unit 10 introduces it; this lesson looks closely at the language).",
      "Formal in this sense means three things:",
      {list:[
        "**Precise.** Each word says exactly what you mean: “increased by 12%” rather than “went up a lot”.",
        "**Written, not spoken.** It avoids features of conversation such as contractions (“don’t”), fillers (“like”, “you know”) and chatty phrases (“at the end of the day”).",
        "**Focused on the ideas.** The writing keeps attention on the evidence and the argument, not on the writer’s feelings or the reader’s daily life."
      ]},
      {key:"Formal does not mean long or complicated words. “Use” is better than “utilise” in most sentences. The goal is precision, not decoration."},
      "Register also changes with the genre (the type of text). A research article, a lab report and an exam essay are usually quite formal. A reflective journal, a blog post or a short email to a classmate can be less formal. This app is written in a plain, friendly style on purpose, with contractions and “you”, because it is a set of lessons, not an essay. The examples in this unit show the style of a **formal academic essay or report**, and every rule here assumes that context."
    ]},
    {h:"Contractions", body:[
      "A [[contraction]] joins two words and replaces some letters with an apostrophe: “don’t” (do not), “it’s” (it is or it has), “they’re” (they are), “won’t” (will not), “can’t” (cannot). Contractions are correct English, and everyone uses them in speech. But most formal academic genres avoid them.",
      {table:[
        ["Contraction","Full form"],
        ["The method doesn’t work for small samples.","The method does not work for small samples."],
        ["It’s been tested in three countries.","It has been tested in three countries."],
        ["Results won’t be available until 2027.","Results will not be available until 2027."],
        ["Farmers can’t afford the fees.","Farmers cannot afford the fees."]
      ]},
      "Notice that “cannot” is usually written as **one word** in modern English.",
      {warn:"Not every apostrophe is a contraction. In “the study’s aim” or “the participants’ answers”, the apostrophe shows possession, and it is perfectly formal. Keep it. Also watch the classic mix-up: “it’s” means “it is” or “it has”, while “its” (no apostrophe) means “belonging to it”: “The committee published its report.”"},
      "Contractions are fine inside direct quotations (copy the speaker’s words exactly) and in less formal genres. If you are unsure, check two or three recent texts of the same type in your field, or ask your tutor."
    ]},
    {h:"Phrasal verbs: which ones to change", body:[
      "A [[phrasal-verb]] is a verb plus a small word such as “up”, “out”, “into” or “off”, often with a meaning you can’t guess from the parts: “come up with” means “think of an idea”, not “come” and “up”. English has thousands of them.",
      {tip:"Grammar books often separate **phrasal verbs** in the strict sense (a verb plus an adverb particle, such as “carry out” or “set up”) from **prepositional verbs** (a verb plus a preposition, such as “focus on”, “rely on” or “look into”). Many learner books and dictionaries call them all phrasal verbs or multi-word verbs, and this lesson does the same, because the style question is the same for both."},
      "They are not all the same. It helps to think of three groups.",
      {h:"1. Normal in academic writing: keep them"},
      "Many multi-word verbs are standard in research articles, often because there is no better single verb: **carry out** (a study), **set up** (a system), **point out**, **focus on**, **rely on**, **consist of**, **account for**, **result in**, **depend on**. Write “The survey was carried out in March” without worrying.",
      {h:"2. Conversational: often better as a single verb"},
      {table:[
        ["Conversational","More formal and precise"],
        ["look into","investigate, examine"],
        ["find out","discover, determine"],
        ["come up with","devise, propose, develop"],
        ["go up / go down","increase, rise / decrease, fall, decline"],
        ["get rid of","eliminate, remove"],
        ["cut down on","reduce"],
        ["put off","postpone, delay"],
        ["talk about","discuss"]
      ]},
      "Choose the single verb that matches your exact meaning. “Rents went up” could become “Rents rose”, “Rents increased” or, if the change was large, “Rents rose sharply”.",
      {h:"3. Context matters"},
      "Some phrasal verbs sit in the middle. “Look at” and “find out” appear in published academic writing too, especially in less formal fields and in spoken presentations. In a formal essay, a single verb is the safer choice, but nobody will fail you for one “look at”.",
      {warn:"**Check the grammar of the new verb.** Single verbs often don’t take the small word of the phrasal verb. You “talk about” a topic but you “discuss” it, with no “about”: “The report discusses water prices”, never “discusses about”. In the same way you “investigate” a problem (not “investigate into”), though you can carry out “an investigation into” it. A [[learner-dictionary]] shows the pattern for each verb."}
    ]},
    {h:"“You” and the writer’s voice", body:[
      "In conversation we often use “you” to mean “people in general”: “If you smoke, you are more likely to get lung cancer.” In a formal essay this general “you” sounds as if you are talking directly to the reader about their own life. Most academic writers rephrase it.",
      {table:[
        ["General “you”","Formal alternatives"],
        ["If you smoke, you are more likely to get lung cancer.","Smoking increases the risk of lung cancer."],
        ["You can see this pattern in Table 2.","This pattern is shown in Table 2. / Table 2 shows this pattern."],
        ["When you move to a new city, you often feel isolated.","People who move to a new city often feel isolated."]
      ]},
      "The best replacement is usually a **specific noun** (smokers, new residents, teachers, readers), a **process noun** (smoking, migration) or a sentence about the evidence itself (“Table 2 shows…”). The pronoun “one” (“One can see…”) is correct but sounds very formal, even old-fashioned, to many readers, so use it rarely.",
      "What about “I” and “we”? This genuinely varies. Many fields, and style guides such as APA, now accept or recommend “I” or “we” for what the authors did (“We interviewed 30 nurses”). Other fields and some tutors prefer impersonal forms (“Thirty nurses were interviewed”). Check your department’s guidance or recent articles in your field. Research Skills covers this in {{academic-style}}."
    ]},
    {h:"Vague and conversational words", body:[
      "Some words are fine in speech because the listener can see what you mean. In writing they leave the reader guessing. Replace them with something specific.",
      {table:[
        ["Vague or chatty","Ask yourself","Possible replacements"],
        ["thing, stuff","Which thing exactly?","factor, method, problem, the cost of transport"],
        ["a lot of, lots of","How many? How much?","many (countable), much (uncountable), a large number of, considerable, or a figure"],
        ["big, good, bad, nice","Big in what way? Good for what?","large, major, substantial; effective, reliable; harmful"],
        ["get","Which meaning?","obtain, receive, become, reach"],
        ["really, very, so","Does it add meaning?","delete it, or use a precise word: “very important” → “essential”, or give a number"],
        ["kind of, sort of","Is this a hedge?","somewhat, partly, to some extent"],
        ["etc., and so on","What else is in the list?","name the items, or write “such as” plus two or three examples"]
      ]},
      "Other conversational habits to avoid in a formal essay: exclamation marks, lots of rhetorical questions (one, well placed, can work), “like” as a filler, and clichés such as “in today’s world” or “since the dawn of time”.",
      {tip:"Watch “much” and “many” when you replace “a lot of”. “Many” goes with countable plurals (many studies, many farmers). “Much” goes with uncountable nouns (much research, much evidence), and in positive statements “much” can sound stiff, so “a great deal of” or “considerable” often reads better: “There is considerable evidence that…”. Lesson 3 explains countable and uncountable nouns."},
      {warn:"“And” or “But” at the start of a sentence is not a grammar mistake, and many good writers do it. In formal essays, though, many tutors prefer “In addition,” or “However,”. Follow your tutor or style guide; it is a style choice, not a rule of English."}
    ]},
    {h:"Spelling: pick one variety and stay with it", body:[
      "English has more than one standard spelling system. The two you will meet most are US and UK spelling. Both are correct. Australian, Canadian, Indian, Nigerian, Singaporean and other varieties each mix them in their own ways. What matters in academic writing is **consistency**: choose one and use it throughout, unless your university, journal or publisher asks for a particular one.",
      {table:[
        ["US","UK"],
        ["analyze, organization","analyse, organisation (or organization)"],
        ["behavior, color","behaviour, colour"],
        ["center, fiber","centre, fibre"],
        ["modeling, traveled","modelling, travelled"],
        ["program (all uses)","programme (a plan or course), program (computer)"]
      ]},
      "UK writers can also choose “-ize” spellings (organize, recognize), sometimes called Oxford spelling, but “analyse” keeps its “-yse” in UK English either way. Set your word processor’s spelling language to the variety you have chosen so it flags the other one.",
      {tip:"Quotations keep their original spelling. If you quote an American source in a UK-spelled essay, don’t “correct” its spelling."}
    ]},
    {h:"Putting it together", body:[
      "Here is how the changes work together. Each step fixes one feature, and the meaning becomes clearer at every step, not just more formal.",
      {fig:'<svg viewBox="0 0 340 352"><defs><marker id="e1u2m1" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path class="d-inkf" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="d-rose" x="10" y="10" width="320" height="52" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="32">Researchers haven’t looked into</text><text x="20" y="51">this stuff much.</text><path class="d-ink" d="M30,64 L30,92" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#e1u2m1)"/><text class="s" x="44" y="83">1 Contraction → full form</text><rect class="d-gold" x="10" y="96" width="320" height="52" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="118">Researchers have not looked into</text><text x="20" y="137">this stuff much.</text><path class="d-ink" d="M30,150 L30,178" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#e1u2m1)"/><text class="s" x="44" y="169">2 Phrasal verb → single verb</text><rect class="d-lup" x="10" y="182" width="320" height="52" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="204">Researchers have not investigated</text><text x="20" y="223">this stuff much.</text><path class="d-ink" d="M30,236 L30,264" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#e1u2m1)"/><text class="s" x="44" y="255">3 Vague words → specific words</text><rect class="d-sage" x="10" y="268" width="320" height="52" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="290">Few studies have investigated the</text><text x="20" y="309">long-term effects of night-shift work.</text><text class="s" x="170" y="342" text-anchor="middle">Same idea, more precise wording</text></svg>', alt:"Four boxes, top to bottom, joined by arrows. Box 1: Researchers haven’t looked into this stuff much. Arrow labelled 1, contraction to full form. Box 2: Researchers have not looked into this stuff much. Arrow labelled 2, phrasal verb to single verb. Box 3: Researchers have not investigated this stuff much. Arrow labelled 3, vague words to specific words. Box 4: Few studies have investigated the long-term effects of night-shift work.", cap:"Three small changes turn a spoken sentence into an academic one. The biggest gain is the last: saying exactly what “this stuff” was."},
      "Notice step 3. “Not … much” became “Few studies”, and “this stuff” became the actual topic. That step needed knowledge of the subject, not just better vocabulary. Formal writing often forces you to decide what you really mean.",
      {table:[
        ["Common belief","Closer to the truth"],
        ["Formal writing uses long, rare words.","It uses precise words. Short common words are often best."],
        ["Phrasal verbs are always wrong.","Many (carry out, focus on) are standard. Change the conversational ones."],
        ["Never write “I”.","It depends on the field and the guide. Many now accept it."],
        ["Formal means passive verbs everywhere.","Use the passive when it helps (Lesson 3), not as a disguise."],
        ["US spelling (or UK spelling) is the correct one.","Both are correct. Be consistent."]
      ]},
      {terms:["contraction","phrasal-verb"]}
    ]}
   ],
   try:[
    {id:"e1u2-l1-x1", type:"mcq", q:"In a formal essay, which sentence is the most academic?", options:[
      {t:"Rents in the city centre rose by 18% between 2019 and 2023.", ok:true, why:"It uses a precise single verb (“rose”), gives the size of the change and the period, and has no conversational features."},
      {t:"Rents in the city centre went up a lot over the last few years.", why:"“Went up” is a conversational phrasal verb, “a lot” is vague, and “the last few years” doesn’t say which years. The reader can’t check or compare the claim."},
      {t:"Rents in the city centre have gone through the roof!", why:"“Through the roof” is an idiom and the exclamation mark is conversational. Neither tells the reader how much rents changed."},
      {t:"You’ll notice that rents in the city centre are kind of high now.", why:"This has a contraction (“you’ll”), the general “you” and a vague hedge (“kind of”). It also gives no figure or date."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l1-x2", type:"match", q:"Match each conversational phrasal verb to a more formal single verb with the same meaning.", pairs:[
      ["look into (a problem)","investigate","Both mean to study something carefully to find out the facts."],
      ["come up with (a plan)","devise","Both mean to create a plan or method by thinking."],
      ["get rid of (a source of error)","eliminate","Both mean to remove something completely."],
      ["cut down on (sugar)","reduce","Both mean to use or have less of something."],
      ["put off (a decision)","postpone","Both mean to move something to a later time."]
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l1-x3", type:"spot", q:"Mei is revising a paragraph for a formal report. Tap every word or phrase that is too **informal** for that context. Some highlighted parts are fine.", ask:"informal", parts:[
      "The project team ",{t:"carried out", flag:false, why:"“Carry out” is a phrasal verb, but it is standard in research writing. Keep it."}," a survey of 200 households. ",{t:"It’s", flag:true, why:"A contraction. In a formal report write “It is”."}," clear that ",{t:"lots of", flag:true, why:"Vague and conversational. Give the number (“143 of the 200 households”) or use “many”."}," families ",{t:"spend", flag:false, why:"A plain, precise verb. Nothing to change."}," more than a third of their income on rent. ",{t:"However", flag:false, why:"A formal transition signal that shows a contrast. Fine."},", ",{t:"you can’t tell", flag:true, why:"Two informal features: the general “you” and a contraction. Rephrase, for example “the data do not show…”."}," from the data ",{t:"why stuff differs", flag:true, why:"“Stuff” is vague and very informal. Name what you mean, for example “why housing costs differ”."}," between districts."
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l1-x4", type:"sort", q:"For a **formal academic essay**, sort each word or phrase: suitable as it is, or better replaced?", buckets:["Suitable","Better replaced"], items:[
      {t:"focus on", b:0, why:"A standard multi-word verb in academic writing: “This chapter focuses on…”."},
      {t:"considerable", b:0, why:"A precise, formal adjective: “considerable variation”."},
      {t:"set up (a monitoring system)", b:0, why:"A normal phrasal verb in research writing. “Establish” is also fine, but “set up” is not wrong."},
      {t:"approximately", b:0, why:"A precise hedge on a number: “approximately 40%”."},
      {t:"kind of", b:1, why:"Conversational. Use “somewhat”, “partly” or “to some extent”, or remove it."},
      {t:"don’t", b:1, why:"A contraction. Write “do not” in a formal essay."},
      {t:"things", b:1, why:"Vague. Name the things: factors, problems, methods, costs…"},
      {t:"you (meaning people in general)", b:1, why:"Rephrase with a specific noun (“patients”, “voters”) or a different structure."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l1-x5", type:"mcq", q:"Kofi wants to replace “talks about” in this sentence: “The report talks about water prices in three regions.” Which version is correct and formal?", options:[
      {t:"The report discusses water prices in three regions.", ok:true, why:"“Discuss” takes a direct object, with no preposition. The subject “the report” is singular, so the verb is “discusses”."},
      {t:"The report discusses about water prices in three regions.", why:"“Discuss” does not take “about”. This is a very common error when a writer swaps “talk about” for “discuss” and keeps the old preposition."},
      {t:"The report discuss water prices in three regions.", why:"“The report” is singular, so the present-tense verb needs -s: “discusses”."},
      {t:"The report is discussing about water prices in three regions.", why:"Two problems: “about” again, and the progressive “is discussing”, which is unusual for describing what a document says. Use the simple present: “discusses”."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l1-x6", type:"mcq", q:"Ana replaces “a lot of” in “There is a lot of evidence that…”. Which version is correct?", options:[
      {t:"There is considerable evidence that…", ok:true, why:"“Evidence” is uncountable, so it takes a singular verb (“is”) and words such as “considerable”, “much” or “a great deal of”."},
      {t:"There are many evidences that…", why:"“Evidence” is uncountable in standard academic English, so it has no plural “evidences” and doesn’t take “many”. Use “many studies” or “several pieces of evidence” if you need to count."},
      {t:"There is many evidence that…", why:"“Many” goes with countable plural nouns (many studies). “Evidence” is uncountable."},
      {t:"There are a considerable evidence that…", why:"Uncountable nouns don’t take “a” or a plural verb. “There is considerable evidence” is correct."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l1-x7", type:"mcq", q:"Priya has written “analyse” on page 1 and “analyze” on page 4 of her essay. What is the best advice?", options:[
      {t:"Both spellings are correct; choose one (or the one your university or journal asks for) and use it throughout.", ok:true, why:"“Analyse” is UK spelling and “analyze” is US spelling. Neither is wrong, but mixing them looks careless."},
      {t:"Change both to “analyze”, because US spelling is the international standard.", why:"There is no single international standard. Both varieties are used in academic publishing; consistency is what matters."},
      {t:"Change both to “analyse”, because UK spelling is more formal.", why:"UK spelling is not more formal than US spelling. They are two equal standards."},
      {t:"Leave them; readers don’t notice spelling.", why:"Inconsistent spelling is easy to notice and can make careful work look rushed. Fixing it takes a minute with a spell checker set to one variety."}
    ]}
   ],
   apply:{intro:"Short tasks with your own writing or a text from your field. Use the notes box for each.", tasks:[
    {t:"Rewrite a paragraph more formally", how:["Find a paragraph you wrote informally: a message, a social media post or an old draft (or write five sentences about your day at work or study, as you would say them to a friend).","Underline every contraction, conversational phrasal verb, general “you” and vague word (thing, stuff, a lot, really, get).","Rewrite the paragraph for a formal report. Use a learner dictionary to check the grammar of each new verb.","Read both versions. Note one place where the formal version forced you to be more precise."], write:"My original… The features I underlined… My formal version… Where I had to be more precise…"},
    {t:"Collect phrasal verbs from your field", how:["Open a free research article or textbook chapter in your subject.","Find five phrasal or multi-word verbs (for example carry out, focus on, consist of, set up).","For each one, write the whole phrase it appears in.","Decide: is it standard here, or could a single verb do the job better?"], write:"1. Phrase from the text… standard or replace?… 2. …"},
    {t:"Choose your spelling variety", how:["Check whether your university, department or target journal states a spelling preference (style guides and author instructions usually say).","Set your word processor’s language to that variety (for example English (United Kingdom) or English (United States)).","Write down five words you often use that differ between US and UK spelling, in your chosen form."], write:"My variety… Where the rule comes from… Five words in my variety…"}
   ], proof:"A before-and-after paragraph, a list of five phrasal verbs from your field with your decision on each, and a note of the spelling variety you will use."},
   read:[
     {book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H5 “Words and Language”: “Slang” and “Technical Expressions”, and section H6 “Point of View”: “Second Person”", note:"Short examples of when slang and “you” fit and when they don’t."},
     {book:"owg", where:"Section 2.7 “Spotlight on … Variations of English”", note:"Background reading on dialects and varieties of English and the right to your own language. Its examples are from the United States."}
   ]
  },
  /* ===================== LESSON 2 ===================== */
  {id:"e1u2-l2", title:"Building clear sentences", minutes:55,
   hook:{q:"Which of these is a complete sentence in formal written English?", options:["Because the sample was small.","The sample was small.","Although the data were limited.","Which made the results hard to generalise."], answer:1, reveal:"**Only “The sample was small.” can stand alone.** The other three are **fragments**: they start with a word (because, although, which) that makes them depend on another idea, so the reader is left waiting for the rest. In speech we say fragments all the time, and they sound fine. In formal writing they look unfinished. This lesson shows how English sentences are built, how to join ideas, and how to fix the most common sentence errors."},
   goals:[
     "Identify clauses, and tell simple, compound and complex sentences apart",
     "Join ideas with coordinators (and, but, so) and subordinators (because, although, whereas) that show the right relationship",
     "Punctuate transition words such as “however” and “therefore” correctly, and fix comma splices and run-on sentences",
     "Fix sentence fragments, and write lists and pairs with parallel structure"
   ],
   learn:[
    {h:"The building block: the clause", body:[
      {lead:"Think of a sentence as a building made of blocks. The basic block is the **clause**."},
      "A [[clause]] is a group of words with a **subject** (who or what the sentence is about) and a **verb that shows tense** (such as “rose”, “is”, “has changed”, “will open”). “Rainfall declined” is a clause. “The decline in rainfall” is not: it has no verb, so it is just a noun phrase.",
      "(Grammar books also describe **non-finite clauses**, built on an -ing form or a “to” infinitive with no tense, such as “showing that demand was high”. They can’t make a sentence on their own. In this lesson, “clause” means a clause with a verb that shows tense, the kind every full sentence needs.)",
      "There are two kinds of clause:",
      {list:[
        "An [[independent-clause]] can stand alone as a sentence: “Rainfall declined.”",
        "A [[dependent-clause]] can’t stand alone. It begins with a word such as “because”, “although”, “when”, “if”, “which” or “who”, and it needs an independent clause to lean on: “because rainfall declined…” leaves the reader waiting for the main point."
      ]},
      "Once you can see the clauses in a sentence, most punctuation decisions and most sentence errors become easy to understand. A useful habit when you check a long sentence: underline each verb that shows tense, then find its subject. Each pair is one clause."
    ]},
    {h:"Simple, compound and complex sentences", body:[
      "Grammar books name sentence types by the clauses they contain.",
      {table:[
        ["Type","What it contains","Example"],
        ["Simple","One independent clause","Rainfall declined between 1990 and 2020."],
        ["Compound","Two or more independent clauses joined by a coordinator or a semicolon","Rainfall declined, and crop yields fell."],
        ["Complex","One independent clause plus one or more dependent clauses","Because rainfall declined, crop yields fell."],
        ["Compound-complex","Two or more independent clauses plus at least one dependent clause","Because rainfall declined, crop yields fell, and many families left the region."]
      ]},
      {warn:"“Simple” does not mean short. “The rapid growth of informal settlements on the edges of large cities has put pressure on water supplies” is a simple sentence: it has only one clause, with one subject (“the rapid growth…”) and one verb (“has put”). Academic English often packs a lot of information into the [[noun-phrase|noun phrases]] of simple sentences ([[nominalisation]] is one way it does this; see {{noun-phrases-nominalisation|Unit 1}})."},
      "Good academic writing mixes all four types. A paragraph made only of short simple sentences feels choppy and hides the links between ideas. A paragraph of very long compound-complex sentences tires the reader. Use the structure that shows how the ideas relate."
    ]},
    {h:"Joining ideas: coordinators and subordinators", body:[
      "To join two independent clauses as equals, use a [[coordinating-conjunction|coordinating conjunction]]. There are seven, sometimes remembered as FANBOYS: **for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so**. When one joins two independent clauses, put a comma before it (writers sometimes leave the comma out when both clauses are very short).",
      {ex:"The clinic opened in 2018, but few patients used it at first.\nThe survey was anonymous, so participants could answer freely.", label:"Coordinators join equal ideas"},
      "To show that one idea depends on another, use a [[subordinator]] (also called a subordinating conjunction). Each subordinator has a precise meaning, so choose it carefully.",
      {table:[
        ["Relationship","Subordinators","Example"],
        ["Reason","because, since, as","Because the road flooded, the clinic closed."],
        ["Contrast or concession","although, even though, while, whereas","Although the sample was small, the results were consistent."],
        ["Condition","if, unless, provided that","Unless funding increases, the programme will end."],
        ["Time","when, after, before, until, once","Once the data were cleaned, the analysis began."],
        ["Purpose","so that","The forms were translated so that all residents could take part."]
      ]},
      "Punctuation: when the dependent clause comes **first**, put a comma after it (some writers leave it out after a very short clause, but the comma is never wrong there). When it comes **second**, you usually don’t need a comma: “The clinic closed because the road flooded.” With contrast words such as “although”, “whereas” and “while”, many writers do use a comma in the second position too.",
      {tip:"Three subordinators have two meanings. “Since” can mean time (“since 2015”) or reason; “while” can mean time or contrast; “as” can mean time or reason. If a reader could take it either way, choose a clearer word: “because” for reason, “whereas” for contrast."}
    ]},
    {h:"Two joining errors to avoid", body:[
      {h:"“Although … but”"},
      "In English, use **one** joining word for one relationship. “Although the sample was small, but the results were consistent” is incorrect, because “although” and “but” both mark the contrast. Choose one: “Although the sample was small, the results were consistent” or “The sample was small, but the results were consistent.” This is a common error for speakers of languages where a pair of words meaning “although … but” is normal, such as Chinese (suīrán … dànshì). The same applies to “because … so”.",
      {h:"“Despite” versus “although”"},
      "“Although” is followed by a clause (subject + verb). “Despite” and “in spite of” are followed by a noun phrase or an -ing form. “Because of” works the same way as “despite”.",
      {table:[
        ["Incorrect","Correct"],
        ["Despite the sample was small, …","Despite the small sample, … / Although the sample was small, …"],
        ["Despite of the cost, …","Despite the cost, … / In spite of the cost, …"],
        ["Because of the road flooded, …","Because the road flooded, … / Because of the flood, …"]
      ]},
      "(“Despite the fact that the sample was small” is also correct, but it is wordy. Lesson 5 is about cutting phrases like that.)"
    ]},
    {h:"Transition words, comma splices and run-ons", body:[
      "Words such as **however, therefore, moreover, furthermore, consequently, nevertheless** and **thus** link ideas, but grammatically they are adverbs, not conjunctions. Grammar books call them [[conjunctive-adverb|conjunctive adverbs]]. The key fact: **they can’t join two independent clauses with only a comma.** Use a full stop or a semicolon before them.",
      {fig:'<svg viewBox="0 0 340 440"><rect class="d-panel" x="10" y="8" width="320" height="56" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="30">Idea A: The sample was small.</text><text x="20" y="52">Idea B: The results were consistent.</text><rect class="d-sage" x="10" y="78" width="320" height="62" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text class="s" x="20" y="96">✓ Comma + coordinator</text><text x="20" y="115">The sample was small, but the</text><text x="20" y="132">results were consistent.</text><rect class="d-sage" x="10" y="150" width="320" height="62" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text class="s" x="20" y="168">✓ Semicolon + transition word + comma</text><text x="20" y="187">The sample was small; however, the</text><text x="20" y="204">results were consistent.</text><rect class="d-sage" x="10" y="222" width="320" height="62" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text class="s" x="20" y="240">✓ Two sentences</text><text x="20" y="259">The sample was small. However, the</text><text x="20" y="276">results were consistent.</text><rect class="d-sage" x="10" y="294" width="320" height="62" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text class="s" x="20" y="312">✓ Subordinator (dependent clause first)</text><text x="20" y="331">Although the sample was small, the</text><text x="20" y="348">results were consistent.</text><rect class="d-rose" x="10" y="370" width="320" height="62" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text class="s" x="20" y="388">✗ Comma splice: a comma alone can’t join</text><text x="20" y="407">The sample was small, however the</text><text x="20" y="424">results were consistent.</text></svg>', alt:"Top box: Idea A, the sample was small; Idea B, the results were consistent. Four correct ways to join them, each in a box with a tick. One: comma plus coordinator, The sample was small, but the results were consistent. Two: semicolon plus transition word plus comma, The sample was small; however, the results were consistent. Three: two sentences, The sample was small. However, the results were consistent. Four: subordinator first, Although the sample was small, the results were consistent. Bottom box with a cross: comma splice, The sample was small, however the results were consistent.", cap:"Four correct ways to join two contrasting ideas, and the common error. “However” is not a conjunction, so a comma alone can’t join the clauses."},
      "A [[comma-splice]] joins two independent clauses with only a comma. A [[run-on-sentence]] (sometimes called a fused sentence) runs them together with no punctuation at all. Some guides use “run-on” for both errors.",
      {table:[
        ["Error","Example","One fix"],
        ["Comma splice","Prices rose, many families moved away.","Prices rose, so many families moved away."],
        ["Comma splice with “however”","The policy was popular, however it was expensive.","The policy was popular; however, it was expensive."],
        ["Run-on","More research is needed it should cover a full year.","More research is needed. It should cover a full year."]
      ]},
      "“However” and its relatives can also sit inside a clause, between commas: “The policy, however, was expensive.” And “therefore” often sits inside a clause with no commas at all: “The sample was therefore small.”",
      {tip:"One exception: when “however” means “in whatever way”, it is not a transition and takes no comma after it: “However the data are analysed, the trend remains.”"}
    ]},
    {h:"Fragments", body:[
      "A [[sentence-fragment]] is a piece of a sentence punctuated as if it were complete. In formal academic writing, fragments are almost always errors. (Journalists and novelists use them on purpose; essays and reports usually don’t.) The three most common kinds:",
      {table:[
        ["Fragment","Why","Fix"],
        ["Because fares were lower.","A dependent clause on its own","Ridership rose more in the second city because fares were lower."],
        ["For example, the rise in rents.","No verb","For example, rents rose sharply."],
        ["Showing that demand was high.","An -ing phrase on its own, with no subject","Queues formed every morning, showing that demand was high."]
      ]},
      "The usual fix is to **attach** the fragment to the sentence before it, or to **add** the missing subject or verb.",
      "Watch “such as” too. It introduces examples inside a sentence (“crops such as maize and cassava”), so a sentence can’t begin with it and stop: “Such as maize and cassava.” is a fragment."
    ]},
    {h:"Parallel structure", body:[
      "[[parallel-structure|Parallel structure]] means that items doing the same job in a sentence have the same grammatical form. It makes lists and comparisons easy to read.",
      {table:[
        ["Not parallel","Parallel"],
        ["The survey measured income, education and whether people owned a car.","The survey measured income, education and car ownership."],
        ["The programme aimed not only to reduce costs but also improving access.","The programme aimed not only to reduce costs but also to improve access."],
        ["Participants were asked to read the text, summarising it and then a discussion.","Participants were asked to read the text, summarise it and then discuss it."]
      ]},
      "Pairs such as **both … and**, **either … or**, **neither … nor** and **not only … but also** need the same form after each part: “both in rural and urban areas” is less balanced than “in both rural and urban areas”.",
      "Parallel structure matters in **bullet lists and headings** too. If one bullet starts with a verb (“Collect the data”), they all should.",
      {tip:"The comma before “and” at the end of a list (“income, education, and car ownership”) is called the serial or Oxford comma. Some style guides, APA among them, require it; others usually leave it out. This unit’s examples leave it out. Both are correct. Follow your guide and be consistent."},
      {terms:["clause","independent-clause","dependent-clause","compound-sentence","complex-sentence","coordinating-conjunction","subordinator","conjunctive-adverb","comma-splice","run-on-sentence","sentence-fragment","parallel-structure"]}
    ]}
   ],
   try:[
    {id:"e1u2-l2-x1", type:"sort", q:"Sort each sentence by type.", buckets:["Simple","Compound","Complex"], items:[
      {t:"Rainfall in the region declined between 1990 and 2020.", b:0, why:"One clause: subject “rainfall”, verb “declined”."},
      {t:"The rapid growth of informal settlements on the edges of large cities has put pressure on water supplies.", b:0, why:"Long, but still one clause: one subject (“the rapid growth…”) and one verb (“has put”). Simple doesn’t mean short."},
      {t:"The clinic opened in 2018, but few patients used it at first.", b:1, why:"Two independent clauses joined by the coordinator “but”."},
      {t:"Prices rose sharply; many families moved to cheaper districts.", b:1, why:"Two independent clauses joined by a semicolon."},
      {t:"Because rainfall declined, crop yields fell.", b:2, why:"A dependent clause (“Because rainfall declined”) plus an independent clause."},
      {t:"Crop yields fell in districts where rainfall declined.", b:2, why:"“Where rainfall declined” is a dependent clause with its own subject and verb, attached to the independent clause."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l2-x2", type:"mcq", q:"Choose the word that fits: “_____ the drug reduced pain, it caused side effects in a third of patients.”", options:[
      {t:"Although", ok:true, why:"The two ideas pull in opposite directions (a benefit, then a cost), so a contrast subordinator fits."},
      {t:"Because", why:"“Because” says the first idea caused the second. Reducing pain didn’t cause the side effects, so the logic would be wrong."},
      {t:"Unless", why:"“Unless” means “if not”, a condition. The sentence reports two facts that both happened."},
      {t:"So that", why:"“So that” shows purpose. Nobody reduced pain in order to cause side effects."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l2-x3", type:"mcq", q:"Which version correctly fixes this comma splice? “The sample was small, however the results were consistent.”", options:[
      {t:"The sample was small; however, the results were consistent.", ok:true, why:"A semicolon can join two independent clauses, and “however” then takes a comma after it. A full stop before “However” would also work."},
      {t:"The sample was small, however, the results were consistent.", why:"Adding a second comma doesn’t help: two independent clauses are still joined by a comma only. “However” is an adverb, not a conjunction."},
      {t:"Although the sample was small, but the results were consistent.", why:"“Although” and “but” both mark the contrast. English uses one: drop “but”."},
      {t:"The sample was small however the results were consistent.", why:"Removing the comma creates a run-on sentence: two independent clauses with nothing joining them."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l2-x4", type:"spot", q:"Tomás drafted this paragraph about bus fares. Tap every sentence that has a **sentence error** (comma splice, run-on or fragment). Some sentences are correct.", ask:"sentence errors", parts:[
      {t:"Tomás compared bus use in two cities over six months.", flag:false, why:"A correct simple sentence."}," ",{t:"Ridership rose in both cities, the rise was larger in the city with lower fares.", flag:true, why:"A comma splice: two independent clauses joined by a comma only. Fix: “…in both cities, but the rise was larger…” or use a semicolon."}," ",{t:"Because fares were lower there.", flag:true, why:"A fragment: a dependent clause standing alone. Attach it to the sentence before, or rewrite it as a full sentence."}," ",{t:"He also interviewed 40 drivers, and most reported more crowded buses at peak times.", flag:false, why:"A correct compound sentence: two independent clauses joined by a comma and “and”."}," ",{t:"Although the study was short, its findings are useful for planners.", flag:false, why:"A correct complex sentence: the dependent clause comes first, followed by a comma."}," ",{t:"More research is needed it should cover a full year.", flag:true, why:"A run-on: two independent clauses with no punctuation or joining word between them. Fix: “More research is needed. It should cover a full year.”"}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l2-x5", type:"mcq", q:"Which sentence is correct?", options:[
      {t:"Despite the small sample, the results were consistent.", ok:true, why:"“Despite” is followed by a noun phrase (“the small sample”). That is its normal pattern."},
      {t:"Despite the sample was small, the results were consistent.", why:"“Despite” can’t be followed by a clause with a subject and verb. Use “Although the sample was small” or “Despite the small sample”."},
      {t:"Despite of the small sample, the results were consistent.", why:"There is no “of” after “despite”. You may be thinking of “in spite of”, which is also correct."},
      {t:"Although the small sample, the results were consistent.", why:"“Although” needs a clause after it: “Although the sample was small”."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l2-x6", type:"mcq", q:"Which sentence has parallel structure?", options:[
      {t:"The workshop helped staff to plan lessons, assess progress and give feedback.", ok:true, why:"All three items in the list are the same form: a base verb plus its object (plan…, assess…, give…)."},
      {t:"The workshop helped staff to plan lessons, assessing progress and feedback.", why:"The list mixes a verb (plan), an -ing form (assessing) and a noun (feedback)."},
      {t:"The workshop helped staff with planning lessons, to assess progress and giving feedback.", why:"The list mixes -ing forms with a “to” infinitive."},
      {t:"The workshop helped staff to plan lessons, progress assessment and to give feedback.", why:"The middle item is a noun phrase between two verbs, so the list is unbalanced."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l2-x7", type:"match", q:"Match each subordinator to the relationship it shows.", pairs:[
      ["whereas","contrast between two facts","“Urban rents rose, whereas rural rents fell.” It sets two facts side by side as opposites."],
      ["unless","condition (if not)","“Unless funding increases, the programme will end” means “if funding does not increase…”."],
      ["so that","purpose","“The forms were translated so that all residents could take part.” It shows why something was done."],
      ["because","reason","It gives the cause of the main clause."],
      ["once","time (as soon as, after)","“Once the data were cleaned, the analysis began.”"]
    ]}
   ],
   apply:{intro:"Practise with your own writing. Each task takes 10–20 minutes.", tasks:[
    {t:"Map the sentences in your own paragraph", how:["Choose a paragraph you have written in English (or write one of about 120 words on a topic you know).","Underline each verb that shows tense and find its subject.","Label each sentence: simple, compound, complex or compound-complex.","If most sentences are the same type, rewrite two so the paragraph shows the links between ideas more clearly."], write:"Sentence types I found… The two sentences I rewrote, before and after…"},
    {t:"Hunt for splices, run-ons and fragments", how:["Read a draft of yours aloud, slowly, one sentence at a time.","Wherever you see “however”, “therefore” or “moreover”, check the punctuation before it.","Check every sentence that starts with because, although, which, such as or an -ing word: is there a main clause?","Fix each problem in one of the ways from this lesson."], write:"Errors found… How I fixed each one…"},
    {t:"Make a list parallel", how:["Find a list in your notes or a draft: a bullet list, a list of aims, or a sentence with three items.","Check whether every item has the same grammatical form.","Rewrite it so that it does (all verbs, all nouns, or all -ing forms)."], write:"My list before… My list after…"}
   ], proof:"A labelled paragraph, a list of sentence errors you found and fixed in your own writing, and one list rewritten with parallel structure."},
   read:[
     {book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H3 “Clear and Effective Sentences” (“Parallelism” and “Variety”) and section H4 “Sentence Errors”", note:"Clear examples of the four sentence types, fragments, comma splices and run-ons, with several ways to fix each."},
     {book:"owg", where:"Section 3.6 “Editing Focus: Sentence Structure”", note:"Practice in combining short sentences into longer ones."}
   ]
  },
  /* ===================== LESSON 3 ===================== */
  {id:"e1u2-l3", title:"Grammar trouble spots", minutes:60,
   hook:{q:"Which of these nouns is usually **uncountable** in academic English, so you would not normally put “a” before it or add -s?", options:["study","result","evidence","finding"], answer:2, reveal:"**Evidence.** You can write “a study”, “two results” and “the main findings”, but not “an evidence” or “evidences”. To count it, say “a piece of evidence” or name what the evidence is: “three studies”. “Research” and “information” work the same way. Countable and uncountable nouns control which articles, verbs and words such as “many” or “much” you use, so this lesson starts there and then moves to articles, agreement, tense and the passive."},
   goals:[
     "Tell countable from uncountable nouns, including research, evidence, information and data",
     "Choose a, an, the or no article using a clear set of questions",
     "Make verbs agree with their real subject, even in long noun phrases",
     "Use the usual tenses for each part of an academic text, knowing that conventions vary by field",
     "Form the passive correctly, use it when it helps, and notice when it hides who acted"
   ],
   learn:[
    {h:"Countable and uncountable nouns", body:[
      {lead:"Some things we count one by one: one study, two studies. Others we measure as a mass: some water, more evidence. English grammar treats these two groups differently, and the difference affects articles, verbs and many small words."},
      "A [[countable-noun]] has a singular and a plural (study / studies, criterion / criteria) and can take “a” or “an”. An **uncountable noun** has no plural, normally doesn’t take “a” or “an”, and takes a singular verb: “The evidence **is** clear.” (A few uncountable nouns can take “a” when an adjective describes a particular kind: “a good knowledge of statistics”, “a high level of education”.)",
      {table:[
        ["Usually uncountable","To count, use…"],
        ["research","a study, studies, research projects"],
        ["evidence","a piece of evidence, or name it: three studies, two documents"],
        ["information","a piece of information, details, facts"],
        ["advice","a piece of advice, recommendations, suggestions"],
        ["equipment","a piece of equipment, instruments, tools"],
        ["feedback","a comment, comments"],
        ["knowledge","(no plural; but note “a good knowledge of statistics”)"]
      ]},
      "Words that go with each group:",
      {table:[
        ["With countable plurals","With uncountable nouns"],
        ["many studies, few studies","much research, little research"],
        ["a large number of studies","a large amount of / a great deal of research"],
        ["fewer errors","less noise"]
      ]},
      "Some nouns are both, with different meanings. **Experience** is uncountable for skill built over time (“five years’ experience in nursing”) and countable for one event (“a difficult experience”). **Work** is uncountable for activity (“much work remains”) and countable for a creation (“the complete works of a writer”). **Paper** is a material (uncountable) but also an article (“a paper on migration”).",
      {h:"Three special cases"},
      {list:[
        "**Research.** In academic English it is usually uncountable. The plural “researches” exists but is rare and many editors change it; write “studies” or “research projects”. (“Research” is also a verb: “She researches coastal erosion.”)",
        "**Data.** Traditionally plural (“the data **are**”; the rare singular is “datum”), and APA Style treats it as plural. Many writers, especially in computing and everyday English, treat it as uncountable (“the data **is**”). Both are widely used. Follow your style guide and be consistent.",
        "**Latin and Greek plurals.** criterion → criteria, phenomenon → phenomena, analysis → analyses, hypothesis → hypotheses, thesis → theses. “Criteria” is plural: “These criteria **are**…”, not “This criteria is…”."
      ]},
      {tip:"[[learner-dictionary|Learner dictionaries]] (such as Cambridge, Oxford, Longman or Collins COBUILD) mark each noun as countable or uncountable, with labels such as [C] and [U] or the words “countable” and “uncountable” (see {{dictionaries-corpora}}). When a noun matters in your field, look it up once and note the label."}
    ]},
    {h:"Articles: a, an, the or nothing", body:[
      "Articles are among the hardest parts of English for many learners, partly because many languages, including Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Russian and Polish, have no articles at all. Others, such as Spanish, French and Arabic, have articles but use them in different places. (Spanish says “El agua es esencial”, with an article, where English says “Water is essential”, without one.) Few native speakers could explain all the rules, but a short set of questions covers most academic sentences. Grammar books call the “nothing” option the **zero article**; see [[article-grammar]].",
      {fig:'<svg viewBox="0 0 340 352"><defs><marker id="e1u2m2" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path class="d-inkf" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="d-panel" x="50" y="8" width="240" height="48" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="170" y="28" text-anchor="middle">Is the noun countable</text><text x="170" y="46" text-anchor="middle">and singular?</text><path class="d-ink" d="M120,56 L88,100" stroke-width="2" fill="none" marker-end="url(#e1u2m2)"/><path class="d-ink" d="M220,56 L252,100" stroke-width="2" fill="none" marker-end="url(#e1u2m2)"/><text class="s" x="82" y="80" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><text class="s" x="268" y="74" text-anchor="middle">no: plural or</text><text class="s" x="268" y="88" text-anchor="middle">uncountable</text><rect class="d-lup" x="10" y="104" width="155" height="52" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="87" y="125" text-anchor="middle">Does the reader</text><text x="87" y="143" text-anchor="middle">know which one?</text><rect class="d-lup" x="175" y="104" width="155" height="52" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="252" y="125" text-anchor="middle">Specific ones the</text><text x="252" y="143" text-anchor="middle">reader can identify?</text><path class="d-ink" d="M60,156 L46,196" stroke-width="2" fill="none" marker-end="url(#e1u2m2)"/><path class="d-ink" d="M114,156 L128,196" stroke-width="2" fill="none" marker-end="url(#e1u2m2)"/><path class="d-ink" d="M225,156 L211,196" stroke-width="2" fill="none" marker-end="url(#e1u2m2)"/><path class="d-ink" d="M279,156 L293,196" stroke-width="2" fill="none" marker-end="url(#e1u2m2)"/><text class="s" x="40" y="178" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><text class="s" x="134" y="178" text-anchor="middle">no</text><text class="s" x="205" y="178" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><text class="s" x="300" y="178" text-anchor="middle">no</text><rect class="d-sage" x="10" y="200" width="74" height="40" rx="6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="47" y="225" text-anchor="middle">the</text><rect class="d-gold" x="91" y="200" width="74" height="40" rx="6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="128" y="225" text-anchor="middle">a / an</text><rect class="d-sage" x="175" y="200" width="74" height="40" rx="6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="212" y="225" text-anchor="middle">the</text><rect class="d-rose" x="256" y="200" width="74" height="40" rx="6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="293" y="225" text-anchor="middle">nothing</text><text class="s" x="47" y="260" text-anchor="middle">the survey</text><text class="s" x="47" y="276" text-anchor="middle">(named before)</text><text class="s" x="128" y="260" text-anchor="middle">a survey</text><text class="s" x="128" y="276" text-anchor="middle">(one of many)</text><text class="s" x="212" y="260" text-anchor="middle">the results</text><text class="s" x="212" y="276" text-anchor="middle">(of this study)</text><text class="s" x="293" y="260" text-anchor="middle">evidence</text><text class="s" x="293" y="276" text-anchor="middle">(in general)</text><text class="s" x="170" y="318" text-anchor="middle">A starting point: names, fixed phrases and</text><text class="s" x="170" y="336" text-anchor="middle">set expressions have their own patterns.</text></svg>', alt:"A decision tree. Top question: is the noun countable and singular? If yes: does the reader know which one? Yes gives the (the survey, named before); no gives a or an (a survey, one of many). If no, meaning plural or uncountable: specific ones the reader can identify? Yes gives the (the results, of this study); no gives no article (evidence, in general). Note: names, fixed phrases and set expressions have their own patterns.", cap:"Two questions decide most articles. A singular countable noun almost always needs a word in front of it."},
      {h:"Use “the” when the reader knows which one"},
      {list:[
        "You have **mentioned it before**: “We conducted a survey. The survey had 20 questions.”",
        "A phrase **after the noun** identifies it: “the effect of temperature on yield”, “the students who completed the survey”.",
        "There is **only one**, or it is obvious from context: “the sun”, “the literature on migration”, “the government” (of the country you are discussing).",
        "With **superlatives and ordinals** (“the largest”, “the first”, “the second”) and with “same” and “only” (“the same method”, “the only study”)."
      ]},
      {h:"Use “a” or “an” for one of many"},
      "Use “a” or “an” for a singular countable noun the reader doesn’t yet know, or to say what kind of thing something is: “Lagos is a large city.” “She works as a nurse.” Choose by **sound**, not spelling: “an hour”, “an MRI scan” (it begins with the sound “em”), but “a university”, “a European study” and “a one-year project” (they begin with a “y” or “w” sound).",
      {h:"Use no article for things in general"},
      "Plural and uncountable nouns used in a general sense take no article: “Water is essential for life.” “Students often underestimate living costs.” “Research on sleep has grown.” Compare a specific case: “**The** water in the village well was contaminated.”",
      {key:"A singular countable noun almost never stands alone. “Survey shows…” is wrong in a sentence; write “A survey shows…”, “The survey shows…” or “Surveys show…”."},
      {warn:"**Names.** Most names of people, cities and countries take no article (Nairobi, Brazil), but plural names and names with words such as “Republic”, “Kingdom” or “States” usually do: the Philippines, the Netherlands, the United Kingdom, the United States. Rivers and oceans take “the” (the Nile, the Pacific). There are also variety differences: UK English says “in hospital” and “at university”, while US English says “in the hospital” and “in college”. Both are correct."}
    ]},
    {h:"Subject–verb agreement", body:[
      "[[subject-verb-agreement|Subject–verb agreement]] means that a singular subject takes a singular verb and a plural subject takes a plural verb. In the present simple, the singular form usually ends in -s: “The study **shows**”, “The studies **show**”. The rule is simple; the trouble is finding the real subject in a long academic sentence.",
      {table:[
        ["Trap","Correct example","Why"],
        ["Long noun phrase","The results of the survey **show**…","The subject is “results”, not “survey”."],
        ["The number / a number","The number of applicants **has** fallen. A number of applicants **have** withdrawn.","“The number” is one figure (singular); “a number of” means “several” (plural)."],
        ["Each, every, one of","Each of the participants **was** paid. One of the main reasons **is** cost.","The subject is “each” or “one”, which is singular."],
        ["There is / there are","There **are** several reasons. There **is** a large body of evidence.","The verb agrees with the noun that follows."],
        ["Uncountable nouns","Research **shows**… Evidence **suggests**…","Uncountable nouns take a singular verb."],
        ["Subjects in -ics","Economics **is** a popular subject. The statistics **show** a rise.","A field of study is singular; “statistics” meaning figures is plural."]
      ]},
      "**Collective nouns** (committee, government, team, staff) usually take a singular verb in US English. In UK English they can take a singular verb when the group acts as one (“The committee **has** decided”) or a plural verb when you think of the members (“The committee **have** different views”). Both patterns are correct; be consistent.",
      {tip:"To check a long sentence, mentally remove everything between the main noun and its verb. In “The quality of the water samples from the three villages was poor”, remove “of the water samples from the three villages” and you are left with “The quality … was poor”: singular subject, singular verb."}
    ]},
    {h:"Tense in academic writing", body:[
      "There is no single rule for [[tense-in-academic-writing|tense in academic writing]], but there are strong conventions. This table shows the pattern in most fields, based on the APA Style guidance on verb tense and the OpenStax *Writing Guide* handbook. Your field or style guide may differ.",
      {table:[
        ["Job in the text","Usual tense","Example"],
        ["General facts and accepted knowledge","Present simple","Malaria is transmitted by mosquitoes."],
        ["What your own text, tables or figures do","Present simple","This essay argues that… Table 3 shows…"],
        ["Research activity up to now (no finished time)","Present perfect","Several studies have examined this question."],
        ["Your method","Past simple","We interviewed 24 nurses. Samples were collected weekly."],
        ["Your results","Past simple","Scores rose in both groups."],
        ["What the results mean","Present simple","These findings suggest that…"]
      ]},
      {h:"Reporting what sources say"},
      "Here fields really differ. In the humanities (and MLA style), writers usually use the **present**: “Okafor argues that…”. In the social and natural sciences (and APA style), writers usually use the **past** or **present perfect**: “Okafor (2021) found that…”, “Researchers have studied…”. (Okafor is an invented name for these examples.) Unit 3 covers [[reporting-verb|reporting verbs]] and their tenses in more detail in {{using-sources-in-language}}.",
      {h:"Past simple or present perfect?"},
      "Use the **past simple** with a finished time: “In 2019, the government introduced a new tax.” Use the **present perfect** for a period that continues to now, or with no time given: “Since 2015, the government **has introduced** three reforms.” “Since 2015, the government introduced…” is a common error.",
      {warn:"Use the present simple for “general facts” carefully. If a claim is new or disputed, report it as a finding of a particular study (“Kim and Osei (2022) found that…”) rather than as a fact everyone accepts. Whatever tenses you choose, keep them consistent within a section."}
    ]},
    {h:"The passive: useful, but watch who disappears", body:[
      "In an **active** sentence, the subject does the action: “The team **measured** the samples.” In a [[passive-voice|passive]] sentence, the thing acted on becomes the subject: “The samples **were measured** (by the team).” The passive is formed with **be + past participle**, and it works in every tense: is measured, was measured, has been measured, will be measured, can be measured.",
      {warn:"Passive is not the same as past. “The samples are stored at −20 °C” is a present passive. “The team measured the samples” is past but active."},
      "The passive is genuinely useful when:",
      {list:[
        "**The actor is unknown, obvious or unimportant.** In a methods section, readers care what was done to the samples, not which assistant did it: “The samples were weighed twice.”",
        "**You want to keep the topic at the start of the sentence.** “The water samples were collected in May. They were then filtered and stored at 4 °C.” (Lesson 4 explains why topic-first order helps readers.)",
        "**You want to distance a claim from yourself:** “It has been suggested that…” (a form of hedging; see Lesson 5)."
      ]},
      "But the passive can also **hide who acted**. “Mistakes were made.” By whom? “Hundreds of residents were evicted.” Who evicted them? In analysis, the actor often matters most. Critical readers ask “by whom?”, and good writers name the actor when responsibility is the point: “The city council evicted hundreds of residents.”",
      "Style guides differ on how much passive to use. APA Style recommends the active voice in most writing and accepts the passive when the thing acted on matters more than the actor. Many science fields still write methods mostly in the passive; others use “we”. Follow your field.",
      {warn:"**Verbs with no object can’t be passive.** happen, occur, arise, exist, emerge, arrive, die and rise describe things that happen without someone doing them to an object. Write “The error occurred during data entry”, not “The error was occurred”. Write “Prices rose”, not “Prices were risen” (or, with an actor, “The company raised prices”)."},
      {terms:["countable-noun","article-grammar","subject-verb-agreement","tense-in-academic-writing","passive-voice"]}
    ]}
   ],
   try:[
    {id:"e1u2-l3-x1", type:"sort", q:"In academic English, is each noun usually countable or uncountable?", buckets:["Countable","Uncountable"], items:[
      {t:"study", b:0, why:"A study, two studies. Use it when you need a countable word for research."},
      {t:"finding", b:0, why:"A finding, the main findings."},
      {t:"criterion", b:0, why:"One criterion, several criteria: a Greek plural, but countable."},
      {t:"evidence", b:1, why:"No “an evidence” or “evidences”; say “a piece of evidence” or name the sources."},
      {t:"information", b:1, why:"No “informations”; say “a piece of information” or “details”."},
      {t:"equipment", b:1, why:"No “equipments”; say “a piece of equipment” or “instruments”."},
      {t:"feedback", b:1, why:"No “a feedback”; say “some feedback” or “comments”."},
      {t:"research (the noun)", b:1, why:"Usually uncountable in academic English. Write “studies” or “research projects” to count."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l3-x2", type:"mcq", q:"Which sentence is correct as a **general** statement?", options:[
      {t:"Access to clean water improves health.", ok:true, why:"All three nouns are uncountable and used in a general sense, so none needs an article."},
      {t:"The access to the clean water improves the health.", why:"“The” makes each noun specific (which access? which water?). For a general statement, no article is needed."},
      {t:"An access to clean water improves health.", why:"“Access” is uncountable here, so it can’t take “an”."},
      {t:"Access to clean water improves the health.", why:"“The health” suggests a specific health the reader already knows about. It would work only with a phrase after it: “the health of young children”."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l3-x3", type:"spot", q:"Hana wrote this paragraph about a farm survey. Tap every **article error** (a missing, extra or wrong article). Some highlighted parts are correct.", ask:"article errors", parts:[
      "In 2024, Hana carried out ",{t:"a survey", flag:false, why:"Correct: the survey is mentioned for the first time, and it is one of many possible surveys."}," of 120 rice farmers. ",{t:"The survey", flag:false, why:"Correct: second mention, so the reader now knows which survey."}," asked about ",{t:"water use", flag:false, why:"Correct: “water use” is uncountable and used in a general sense here, so it needs no article."},". Most farmers said that ",{t:"an irrigation", flag:true, why:"“Irrigation” is uncountable, so it can’t take “an”: “irrigation was their largest cost”."}," was their largest cost. ",{t:"The farmers", flag:false, why:"Correct: the phrase after it (“who used…”) identifies which farmers."}," who used ",{t:"drip system", flag:true, why:"“System” is a singular countable noun, so it can’t stand alone: “who used a drip system” or “who used drip systems”."}," reported lower costs. ",{t:"Survey", flag:true, why:"A singular countable noun can’t stand alone: “The survey also showed…”."}," also showed that ",{t:"information", flag:false, why:"Correct: uncountable and general, so no article."}," about new methods was hard to find."
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l3-x4", type:"mcq", q:"Which sentence has correct subject–verb agreement?", options:[
      {t:"The number of applicants has fallen since 2020.", ok:true, why:"“The number” is a single figure, so it takes a singular verb (“has”)."},
      {t:"The results of the survey shows a clear pattern.", why:"The subject is “results” (plural), not “survey”. Write “The results of the survey show…”."},
      {t:"Each of the participants were given a voucher.", why:"The subject is “each”, which is singular in formal writing: “Each of the participants was given…”."},
      {t:"There is several reasons for this change.", why:"After “there”, the verb agrees with the noun that follows. “Reasons” is plural: “There are several reasons…”."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l3-x5", type:"mcq", q:"Lucía is writing the methods section of a report about a study she completed last year. Which sentence fits best?", options:[
      {t:"We interviewed 24 nurses in two hospitals.", ok:true, why:"The method is finished, so the past simple fits. (In fields that prefer the passive: “Twenty-four nurses were interviewed…”.)"},
      {t:"We interview 24 nurses in two hospitals.", why:"The present simple suggests a general habit, not a completed study. Methods are usually reported in the past."},
      {t:"We have interviewed 24 nurses in 2025.", why:"The present perfect can’t be used with a finished time such as “in 2025”. Use the past simple: “In 2025, we interviewed…”."},
      {t:"We will interview 24 nurses in two hospitals.", why:"The future fits a proposal for a study not yet done. Lucía’s study is finished."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l3-x6", type:"sort", q:"In most academic fields, which tense is usual for each job?", buckets:["Present simple","Past simple","Present perfect"], items:[
      {t:"Saying what a table in your essay shows", b:0, why:"Your table exists now for the reader: “Table 2 shows…”."},
      {t:"Stating a general, widely accepted fact", b:0, why:"General truths are timeless: “Malaria is transmitted by mosquitoes.”"},
      {t:"Explaining what your findings mean", b:0, why:"Interpretation is current: “These results suggest…”."},
      {t:"Describing the steps of your completed study", b:1, why:"Your method happened at a finished time: “We collected…”."},
      {t:"Reporting your own results", b:1, why:"Results are what happened in your study: “Scores rose…”."},
      {t:"Describing research activity from the past up to now, with no finished time", b:2, why:"“Several studies have examined…” connects past research to the present state of knowledge."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l3-x7", type:"mcq", q:"Which sentence is correct?", options:[
      {t:"The error occurred during data entry.", ok:true, why:"“Occur” has no object, so it is used only in the active: “The error occurred”."},
      {t:"The error was occurred during data entry.", why:"“Occur” can’t be passive, because nobody “occurs” an error. Drop “was”."},
      {t:"The error was happened during data entry.", why:"“Happen” is like “occur”: it has no object and no passive. Write “The error happened…”."},
      {t:"The error is occurred during data entry.", why:"Again, “occur” has no passive form. Use the past simple active: “occurred”."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l3-x8", type:"mcq", q:"A news report says: “Hundreds of residents were evicted last month.” What important question does the passive leave unanswered?", options:[
      {t:"Who evicted them?", ok:true, why:"The passive lets the writer leave out the actor. Here the actor (a landlord, a company, the city?) is the key fact for judging responsibility."},
      {t:"When did it happen?", why:"The sentence says “last month”."},
      {t:"How many people were affected?", why:"The sentence says “hundreds of residents”, so an approximate number is given."},
      {t:"Did it happen in the past?", why:"“Were evicted” is past, so the tense already tells you. Passive doesn’t hide time, only (sometimes) the actor."}
    ]}
   ],
   apply:{intro:"These tasks build your own grammar reference. Keep the notes; you will use them for years.", tasks:[
    {t:"Check ten nouns from your field", how:["List ten nouns you use often in your subject (for example: data, research, literature, equipment, analysis, feedback, software).","Look each one up in a free learner dictionary and note whether it is countable [C], uncountable [U] or both.","For each uncountable one, write a countable way to talk about one item (a study, a piece of equipment)."], write:"Noun… C / U / both… countable alternative…"},
    {t:"Audit the articles in one paragraph", how:["Take a paragraph you have written.","Circle every noun. For each singular countable noun, check it has a word in front (a, an, the, this, our…).","For each “the”, ask: does the reader know which one? For each noun without an article, ask: is it plural or uncountable, and general?","Correct anything that fails, then ask a tutor or writing centre to check the doubtful ones."], write:"Changes I made and the reason for each…"},
    {t:"Find the tense pattern in a real article", how:["Open a free research article in your field.","In the introduction, methods, results and discussion, copy one sentence each that reports something.","Note the tense of each main verb, and whether the methods use the passive or “we”.","Compare with the table in this lesson. Does your field follow it?"], write:"Introduction… Methods… Results… Discussion… My field’s pattern…"}
   ], proof:"A personal list of ten field nouns marked C or U, one paragraph with its articles checked, and a note of the tense pattern in your field."},
   read:[
     {book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H7 “Verbs”: “Subject-Verb Agreement” and “Verb Tense in Academic Writing”", note:"Agreement traps with more examples, and how tense in signal phrases differs between the humanities and the sciences."},
     {book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H3 “Clear and Effective Sentences”: “Active Voice”", note:"A short case for the active voice, with an example rewrite."},
     {book:"owg", where:"Section 6.6 “Editing Focus: Subject-Verb Agreement”", note:"Practice identifying subjects and verbs."}
   ]
  },
  /* ===================== LESSON 4 ===================== */
  {id:"e1u2-l4", title:"Paragraphs that hold together", minutes:55,
   hook:{q:"Two paragraphs contain exactly the same facts, but readers find one much easier to follow. What is most likely to be different?", options:["The easier one uses longer, more academic words","The order of information and the links between sentences","The easier one uses more passive verbs","The easier one has more sentences"], answer:1, reveal:"**Order and links.** Readers follow a paragraph best when each sentence starts from something they already know and moves to something new, and when words such as “this”, “however” and “as a result” show exactly how the ideas connect. Linguists call these qualities **cohesion** and **coherence**. This lesson shows how to build paragraphs that have them."},
   goals:[
     "Build a paragraph from a topic sentence, supporting sentences and a concluding or linking sentence",
     "Write topic sentences with a clear controlling idea, and keep every sentence on that idea",
     "Explain the difference between cohesion and coherence, and use given-to-new order, clear reference and repeated key terms",
     "Choose transition signals by their exact meaning, including the differences between however, therefore and moreover"
   ],
   learn:[
    {h:"One paragraph, one main idea", body:[
      {lead:"A paragraph is like a room in a house: it has one main purpose, and everything in it should belong there. A kitchen with a bed in it confuses visitors."},
      "In academic writing, a paragraph develops **one main idea**. A common shape has three parts:",
      {list:[
        "A [[topic-sentence]], usually first, that states the main idea.",
        "[[supporting-sentence|Supporting sentences]] that explain, give evidence and examples, and show why the evidence matters.",
        "A [[concluding-sentence]] that sums up the point or links it to the next paragraph."
      ]},
      {ex:"[Topic sentence] Community health workers have improved access to basic care in many rural areas.\n[Support: what they do] They visit homes, treat common illnesses and refer serious cases to clinics.\n[Support: why it works] Because they live in the communities they serve, they often know which families need help and are trusted by them.\n[Support: evidence] [A cited study or report would go here, for example figures on clinic referrals before and after a programme began.]\n[Concluding or linking sentence] Their success, however, depends partly on steady pay and supervision, which the next section examines.", label:"Worked example: the parts of a paragraph"},
      "Research Skills Unit 10 describes the same shape as four moves (topic sentence, evidence, analysis, link) in {{argument-structure}}. The labels differ slightly between textbooks; the idea is the same. This lesson focuses on the language that makes the parts fit together.",
      {tip:"Paragraph length varies by genre and field. In most academic essays a body paragraph has several sentences: enough to state, support and explain one point. A one-sentence paragraph is rare in formal essays; very long paragraphs usually contain two ideas and can be split."}
    ]},
    {h:"Topic sentences with a controlling idea", body:[
      "A good topic sentence has two parts: the **topic** (what the paragraph is about) and the **controlling idea** (what you will say about it). In “Community health workers have improved access to basic care in many rural areas”, the topic is community health workers and the controlling idea is that they have improved access.",
      {table:[
        ["Problem","Example","Better"],
        ["Too broad","Health is important.","Community health workers have improved access to basic care in many rural areas."],
        ["Too narrow (just one fact)","In 2023, the district had 40 health workers.","The district’s health worker programme grew quickly but unevenly between 2018 and 2023."],
        ["An announcement","This paragraph will talk about health workers.","Health workers are most effective when they are recruited from the communities they serve."]
      ]},
      "The controlling idea is a promise to the reader. Every sentence that follows should keep it. If you notice the paragraph moving to a new idea, either start a new paragraph or rewrite the topic sentence."
    ]},
    {h:"Unity: everything on the main idea", body:[
      "A paragraph has **unity** when every sentence supports its controlling idea. The most common problem is **drift**: a sentence that is true and interesting but belongs somewhere else.",
      "Supporting sentences can do several jobs: explain the idea, give evidence (data, a cited source), give an example, compare, or answer an obvious objection. What they can’t do is start a new topic.",
      {ex:"Community health workers have improved access to basic care in many rural areas. They visit homes and treat common illnesses. [Drift →] Hospitals in capital cities have also opened new specialist wards. [← Drift] Because they live in the communities they serve, they often know which families need help.", label:"Spot the drift"},
      "The marked sentence may be true, but it is about city hospitals, not rural health workers. It breaks the paragraph’s promise.",
      {h:"Concluding and linking sentences"},
      "A concluding sentence should not just repeat the topic sentence in other words. It can say why the point matters (“This suggests that…”), qualify it (“This effect, however, was limited to…”), or link forward (“…which the next section examines”). In a short paragraph inside a longer essay, a link to the next paragraph is often more useful than a summary."
    ]},
    {h:"Cohesion and coherence", body:[
      "These two words sound alike and are often confused.",
      {list:[
        "[[cohesion|Cohesion]] is about the **language links** between sentences: pronouns, repeated words, “this” plus a noun, and transition signals. You can point to cohesion in the text.",
        "[[coherence|Coherence]] is about whether the **ideas make sense together** for the reader: a clear main point, a logical order, and no gaps in the reasoning."
      ]},
      "The classic book on the first idea is M. A. K. Halliday and Ruqaiya Hasan’s *Cohesion in English* (1976), which describes the different kinds of tie between sentences. The most useful ones for academic writers are in this table. Each one is a [[cohesive-device]].",
      {table:[
        ["Device","Example"],
        ["Reference (pronouns, this/these + noun)","The council raised fares. **This increase** reduced ridership."],
        ["Repetition of a key term","…**water use**. **Water use** rose fastest in…"],
        ["Related word forms (see [[word-family|word families]])","Prices **rose** sharply. The **rise** was largest in…"],
        ["Substitution and ellipsis","Some districts introduced fees; others did not (introduce fees)."],
        ["Transition signals","**However**, … **As a result**, …"]
      ]},
      "Cohesion helps coherence, but it is not enough on its own. This passage is full of cohesive links, yet it makes no sense as a paragraph: “My project is about rice. Rice is usually white. White is the colour of snow. Snow falls in winter.” Each sentence connects to the one before, but there is no main idea.",
      {warn:"In academic writing, keep a key term the same throughout. If you write “participants” in one sentence and “subjects”, “respondents” and “people” in the next three, readers may think you mean four different groups. Changing words for variety is a habit from some school essays; in academic English, consistency usually wins."}
    ]},
    {h:"Given to new", body:[
      "Readers of English usually expect a sentence to begin with something they already know (**given** information) and to end with something new. The new information at the end of one sentence then becomes the given starting point of the next. Linguists call this [[given-new|given-to-new]] order.",
      {fig:'<svg viewBox="0 0 340 336"><defs><marker id="e1u2m3" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path class="d-inkf" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker></defs><text x="75" y="22" text-anchor="middle">Given</text><text x="245" y="22" text-anchor="middle">New</text><rect class="d-lup" x="10" y="34" width="130" height="56" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="75" y="58" text-anchor="middle">Many coastal</text><text x="75" y="76" text-anchor="middle">towns</text><rect class="d-gold" x="160" y="34" width="170" height="56" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="245" y="58" text-anchor="middle">depend on</text><text x="245" y="76" text-anchor="middle">fishing.</text><path class="d-ink" d="M230,90 L100,122" stroke-width="2" fill="none" marker-end="url(#e1u2m3)"/><rect class="d-lup" x="10" y="126" width="130" height="56" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="75" y="159" text-anchor="middle">Fishing</text><rect class="d-gold" x="160" y="126" width="170" height="56" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="245" y="150" text-anchor="middle">is threatened by</text><text x="245" y="168" text-anchor="middle">warmer seas.</text><path class="d-ink" d="M230,182 L100,214" stroke-width="2" fill="none" marker-end="url(#e1u2m3)"/><rect class="d-lup" x="10" y="218" width="130" height="56" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="75" y="251" text-anchor="middle">Warmer seas</text><rect class="d-gold" x="160" y="218" width="170" height="56" rx="8" stroke-width="2"/><text x="245" y="242" text-anchor="middle">can push fish into</text><text x="245" y="260" text-anchor="middle">cooler waters.</text><text class="s" x="170" y="304" text-anchor="middle">The new idea at the end of each sentence</text><text class="s" x="170" y="322" text-anchor="middle">becomes the starting point of the next.</text></svg>', alt:"Three sentences shown as rows, each split into a Given box on the left and a New box on the right. Row 1: Many coastal towns / depend on fishing. Row 2: Fishing / is threatened by warmer seas. Row 3: Warmer seas / can push fish into cooler waters. Arrows run from each New box to the Given box of the next row.", cap:"A given-to-new chain. Each sentence picks up the last one’s new idea, so the reader never has to jump."},
      "Compare a version that breaks the chain: “Many coastal towns depend on fishing. Warmer seas are threatening it. Cooler waters are where fish can be pushed by warmer seas.” The facts are the same, but the reader keeps meeting new information first and has to work out the links.",
      "Two other tools help keep the order:",
      {list:[
        "**This/these + a summary noun** at the start of a sentence picks up the whole previous idea: “This decline…”, “These findings…”, “This approach…”.",
        "**The passive** can move the given information to the front: “Fishing is threatened by warmer seas” keeps “fishing” first, where the active (“Warmer seas threaten fishing”) would not."
      ]}
    ]},
    {h:"Pronouns and “this”: make the reference clear", body:[
      "Pronouns (it, they, this, these) save repetition, but only if the reader knows what they refer to.",
      {table:[
        ["Unclear","Clear"],
        ["The council cut the bus budget and raised fares. This angered residents.","…raised fares. These fare rises angered residents."],
        ["The report compared the new policy with the old one, and it was criticised.","…with the old one, and the report was criticised."]
      ]},
      "A good habit: after “this” or “these” at the start of a sentence, add a noun that names what you mean. “This” alone often forces the reader to look back and guess.",
      {tip:"For a person whose gender is unknown or not relevant, APA Style endorses singular “they”, and many other style guides accept it: “Each participant signed the form before they began.” Another option is to make the subject plural: “Participants signed the form before they began.”"}
    ]},
    {h:"Transition signals with exact meanings", body:[
      "A [[transition-signal]] tells the reader how the next idea relates to the last one. Each one makes a specific logical claim, so a wrong one is worse than none. (Research Skills calls this [[signposting]]; Unit 4 of this elective looks at the spoken versions used in lectures, [[lecture-signpost|lecture signposts]].)",
      {table:[
        ["Relationship","Signals","What it claims"],
        ["Addition","moreover, furthermore, in addition, also","Here is another point in the same direction."],
        ["Contrast","however, in contrast, by contrast, on the other hand","The next idea differs from or pulls against the last."],
        ["Concession","nevertheless, nonetheless, even so","The next idea is true despite what was just said."],
        ["Result","therefore, thus, consequently, as a result, hence","The next idea follows from the last."],
        ["Example","for example, for instance","Here is a specific case of the general point."],
        ["Restatement","in other words, that is","Here is the same idea said more clearly."],
        ["Summary","in summary, overall, in conclusion","Here is the point of what came before."]
      ]},
      "See how one pair of facts changes meaning with each signal: “The new vaccine is cheap and easy to store.”",
      {list:[
        "“**However,** uptake has been low.” (a contrast: you would expect high uptake)",
        "“**Therefore,** it is well suited to remote clinics.” (a result that follows from cheapness and easy storage)",
        "“**Moreover,** it requires only one dose.” (another advantage, in the same direction)"
      ]},
      {h:"Pairs that are easy to confuse"},
      {list:[
        "**In contrast** compares two different things: “Urban rents rose by 20%. In contrast, rural rents fell slightly.” **On the contrary** rejects the statement just made: “The policy did not reduce costs. On the contrary, costs rose.”",
        "**On the other hand** introduces the other side of the same question (often after a point “on the one hand”). It does not mean “in addition”.",
        "**For example** can begin a full sentence: “For example, rents rose sharply.” **Such as** introduces a noun phrase inside a sentence: “cities such as Accra and Lima”."
      ]},
      {warn:"Don’t start every sentence with a transition signal. Well-ordered sentences and clear reference often show the links already. Use a signal where the relationship is not obvious, or where you need to make it explicit."},
      {terms:["supporting-sentence","concluding-sentence","cohesion","coherence","cohesive-device","given-new","transition-signal"]}
    ]}
   ],
   try:[
    {id:"e1u2-l4-x1", type:"mcq", q:"Which is the best topic sentence for a paragraph about why a city’s bike-sharing scheme succeeded?", options:[
      {t:"The scheme succeeded mainly because its stations were placed near bus and train stops.", ok:true, why:"It names the topic (the scheme) and a clear controlling idea (it succeeded because of where stations were placed). The rest of the paragraph can support that one point."},
      {t:"Transport is an important issue in cities.", why:"Too broad: it could open thousands of different paragraphs and doesn’t say what this one will argue."},
      {t:"The scheme had 120 stations in 2024.", why:"Too narrow: one fact, with no idea for the paragraph to develop. It might work as a supporting sentence."},
      {t:"This paragraph will discuss the bike-sharing scheme.", why:"An announcement of the topic. It doesn’t say what the writer thinks about the scheme."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l4-x2", type:"spot", q:"Tap every sentence that **drifts** away from the paragraph’s main idea. The other sentences belong.", ask:"drifting sentences", parts:[
      {t:"School meal programmes can improve attendance in low-income areas.", flag:false, why:"The topic sentence: topic (school meal programmes) and controlling idea (they can improve attendance)."}," ",{t:"When a free lunch is available, some families are more likely to send children to school every day.", flag:false, why:"It explains how meals could affect attendance. On topic."}," ",{t:"The history of school buildings in Europe goes back several centuries.", flag:true, why:"Drift: it is about buildings in Europe, not meals and attendance."}," ",{t:"Programmes that buy food from local farmers may also support the local economy.", flag:true, why:"Possibly true and interesting, but it is about the economy, not attendance. It belongs in a different paragraph."}," ",{t:"Several evaluations have reported higher attendance after meal programmes began.", flag:false, why:"Evidence for the controlling idea. On topic (a real paragraph would cite the evaluations)."}," ",{t:"The effect, however, appears to depend on whether meals are served reliably.", flag:false, why:"A qualification of the main idea. On topic, and a good concluding sentence."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l4-x3", type:"order", q:"Put these sentences in order to make a coherent paragraph. Use the topic sentence, the given-to-new links and the transition signals as clues.", items:[
      {t:"Rainwater tanks can make schools in dry regions less dependent on piped water.", why:"First: the topic sentence states the main idea."},
      {t:"A tank collects water from the school roof during the rainy season.", why:"Second: it explains how a tank works, picking up “rainwater tanks” from the topic sentence."},
      {t:"This stored water can then be used for cleaning and gardening in the dry months.", why:"Third: “This stored water” refers back to the water collected in the previous sentence."},
      {t:"However, tank water usually needs treatment before it is safe to drink, which adds cost.", why:"Fourth: “However” introduces a limitation after the benefits."},
      {t:"Tanks are therefore most useful as a supplement to piped supplies, not a replacement.", why:"Last: “therefore” draws the conclusion from the benefit and the limitation together."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l4-x4", type:"match", q:"Match each transition signal to the relationship it claims.", pairs:[
      ["However,","contrast with the previous idea","It signals that the next idea pulls against the last one."],
      ["Therefore,","a result or conclusion","It claims the next idea follows logically from the last."],
      ["Moreover,","another point in the same direction","It adds a further point that supports the same argument."],
      ["For instance,","a specific example","It introduces a case of the general point."],
      ["In other words,","the same idea restated","It rephrases the previous point more clearly or simply."],
      ["Nevertheless,","true despite what was just said","It concedes the previous point but keeps the claim."]
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l4-x5", type:"mcq", q:"Choose the signal that fits: “The vaccine was cheap and easy to store. _____, uptake remained low in the first year.”", options:[
      {t:"However", ok:true, why:"Cheap and easy to store would lead you to expect high uptake, so the low uptake is a contrast."},
      {t:"Therefore", why:"“Therefore” would claim that cheapness caused low uptake, which doesn’t make sense."},
      {t:"Moreover", why:"“Moreover” adds another point in the same direction, but low uptake is not another advantage."},
      {t:"For example", why:"Low uptake is not an example of being cheap and easy to store."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l4-x6", type:"mcq", q:"Choose the signal that fits: “Urban rents rose by 20%. _____, rural rents fell slightly.”", options:[
      {t:"In contrast", ok:true, why:"The sentence compares two different things (urban and rural rents) that moved in opposite directions."},
      {t:"On the contrary", why:"“On the contrary” rejects the previous statement. Nobody is denying that urban rents rose; the writer is comparing."},
      {t:"Therefore", why:"Falling rural rents don’t follow logically from rising urban rents."},
      {t:"Moreover", why:"“Moreover” adds a point in the same direction, but these trends go in opposite directions."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l4-x7", type:"mcq", q:"“The council cut the bus budget and raised fares. This angered residents.” Which revision makes the reference clearest?", options:[
      {t:"The council cut the bus budget and raised fares. These fare rises angered residents.", ok:true, why:"“These fare rises” names exactly what angered residents. Adding a noun after “this” or “these” removes the guesswork."},
      {t:"The council cut the bus budget and raised fares. It angered residents.", why:"“It” could mean the budget cut, the fare rise or the council. The reference is still unclear."},
      {t:"The council cut the bus budget and raised fares. This angered residents a lot.", why:"“A lot” is informal and doesn’t solve the problem: what does “this” refer to?"},
      {t:"The council cut the bus budget and raised fares. That angered residents.", why:"“That” has the same problem as “this”: it doesn’t say which action caused the anger."}
    ]}
   ],
   apply:{intro:"Work on paragraphs from your own writing or from a text in your field.", tasks:[
    {t:"Write a 150-word paragraph and check it", how:["Choose a topic you know well (from your work, studies or community).","Write a topic sentence with a clear controlling idea.","Add three or four supporting sentences and a concluding or linking sentence (about 150 words in total).","Check it: Does every sentence support the controlling idea? Does each sentence start from something given? Is every “this” followed by a noun? Is every transition signal accurate?"], write:"My paragraph… What the check found… What I changed…"},
    {t:"Map the links in a published paragraph", how:["Choose one paragraph from a free article or textbook in your field.","Underline the topic sentence.","Circle every cohesive device: pronouns, this/these + noun, repeated key terms and transition signals.","Draw arrows from each pronoun or “this” to what it refers to."], write:"The topic sentence… The devices I found… One link I would make clearer…"},
    {t:"Start a transition-signal list", how:["Open the free Academic Phrasebank (phrasebank.manchester.ac.uk): its pages “Comparing and contrasting”, “Explaining causality” and “Giving examples” list linking words between sentences. Open a free article in your field too.","Collect eight signals you would really use, two for each of: addition, contrast, result, example (the table in this lesson gives addition signals).","Write one sentence of your own for each, about your subject."], write:"Addition… Contrast… Result… Example…"}
   ], proof:"A 150-word paragraph you have checked, one published paragraph with its links mapped, and a personal list of eight transition signals with your own example sentences."},
   read:[
     {book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H2 “Paragraphs and Transitions”", note:"Effective paragraphs (main point, evidence and analysis, opening and closing paragraphs), flow and a table of transitional words and phrases."},
     {book:"owg", where:"Section 10.6 “Editing Focus: Paragraphs and Transitions”", note:"Practice using transitions in an argument."},
     {book:"phrasebank", where:"“Comparing and contrasting” (“Indicating difference across two sentences”) and “Explaining causality” (“Sentence connectors indicating causality”)", note:"Sentence-level signals for contrast and result."},
     {book:"phrasebank", where:"“Signalling transition”", note:"Phrases for previewing, moving between sections and summarising: transitions between larger parts of a text."}
   ]
  },
  /* ===================== LESSON 5 ===================== */
  {id:"e1u2-l5", title:"Hedging, boosting and concise writing", minutes:55,
   hook:{q:"How many separate hedges (words that make a claim more cautious) are in this sentence? “These findings might possibly suggest a link between diet and sleep.”", options:["One","Two","Three","Four"], answer:2, reveal:"**Three: “might”, “possibly” and “suggest”.** Each one softens the claim, and together they make the writer sound unsure even about what they found. One well-chosen hedge is usually enough: “These findings suggest a link between diet and sleep.” This lesson goes deeper into the grammar of hedges and their opposites, boosters, and then into writing concisely: saying exactly what you mean in as few words as clarity allows."},
   goals:[
     "Use modal verbs, verbs such as “appear to”, adverbs, adjectives and phrases such as “it is likely that” to hedge a claim, with correct grammar",
     "Use boosters for claims the evidence strongly supports, and avoid over-claiming",
     "Decide where a hedge is needed and where plain reporting is better, and fix over-hedging",
     "Cut empty phrases, redundancy and nominalisation overload without cutting hedges, definitions or limitations"
   ],
   learn:[
    {h:"Why academic writers hedge and boost", body:[
      {lead:"Imagine telling a doctor about a pain. “It always hurts” and “It sometimes hurts after I run” lead to different questions. Academic writers make the same kind of choice in every claim: how sure am I, and how sure should my reader be?"},
      "The way writers show their attitude and certainty is called [[stance]] (Unit 1 introduces it for reading in {{reading-critically-language}}). Research Skills introduced [[hedging]]: cautious language that keeps a claim no stronger than the evidence. Its opposite is a [[booster]]: a word that makes a claim stronger and more certain, such as “clearly” or “demonstrates”.",
      "The linguist Ken Hyland has studied hedges and boosters in academic writing for decades, for example in *Hedging in Scientific Research Articles* (1998) and *Metadiscourse: Exploring Interaction in Writing* (2005). One of his central points is that hedging is not weakness or vagueness. In simple terms, it does three useful jobs:",
      {list:[
        "**Accuracy.** It shows exactly how strong the evidence is.",
        "**Honesty.** It marks the limits of a study before a critical reader has to point them out.",
        "**Respect for readers.** It leaves room for other researchers to judge, test and disagree, which is how academic conversation works."
      ]},
      "The rest of this lesson is the language toolkit: which words hedge or boost, how their grammar works, and where to use them."
    ]},
    {h:"Modal verbs: grammar and meaning", body:[
      "A [[modal-verb]] is a small helping verb that adds meaning such as possibility, ability or obligation: **can, could, may, might, will, would, shall, should, must**. Modals are the most common way to hedge.",
      {h:"The grammar is strict"},
      {list:[
        "A modal is followed by the **base form** of a verb, with no “to”: “Higher temperatures **may increase** the risk.” Not “may increases”, “may to increase” or “may increased”.",
        "Modals never take -s: “It **may** rise”, not “It mays rise”.",
        "For possibility in the past, use modal + **have** + past participle: “The flood **may have damaged** the road.”",
        "For the passive, use modal + **be** + past participle: “The effect **could be explained** by…”."
      ]},
      {table:[
        ["Modal","Typical meaning in academic writing","Example"],
        ["can","general possibility: this sometimes happens","Floods can damage roads."],
        ["may, might, could","possibility in a particular case","The decline may reflect lower rainfall."],
        ["would","tentative or hypothetical","This would suggest that the effect is small."],
        ["will","confident prediction","Demand will rise as the population grows."],
        ["must","strong deduction (a booster)","The error must have occurred during transfer."],
        ["should","recommendation, or expectation","Future studies should include rural areas."]
      ]},
      {tip:"Many writers feel that “might” is slightly more tentative than “may”, and “could” is close to both. The differences are small and depend on context, so don’t worry about a precise ranking. What matters more is choosing one modal, not three."}
    ]},
    {h:"More hedging tools", body:[
      "Modals are not the only way to hedge. Academic writers combine several kinds of word. The Academic Phrasebank’s “Being cautious” page groups them by job: distancing the writer from a claim, giving explanations and results cautiously, advising caution, and avoiding over-generalisation.",
      {table:[
        ["Kind","Examples","Grammar to watch"],
        ["Verbs","appear to, seem to, tend to, suggest, indicate","“Income **appears to affect** health.” / “It appears that income affects health.”"],
        ["Adverbs","possibly, probably, perhaps, generally, often, largely, partly","Usually before the main verb or after “be”: “This is probably due to…”"],
        ["Adjectives in it-clauses","it is likely / possible / unlikely that…","“It is likely that rents will rise.” / “Rents are likely to rise.”"],
        ["Nouns","one possibility is that…, there is some evidence that…","Follow with a full clause."],
        ["Quantifiers and approximators","some, many, most, approximately, around, in most cases","“Approximately 40% of…”, “in most cases”"],
        ["Limiting phrases","in this sample, to some extent, under these conditions","They say where the claim applies."]
      ]},
      {warn:"**“Likely” has two patterns.** “Rents are likely to rise” and “It is likely that rents will rise” are correct everywhere. “Likely” is also used as an adverb (“Rents will likely rise”), especially in North American English; some UK editors prefer “will probably rise” or “will most likely rise”. All of these are correct; the main error to avoid is “Rents are likely rise” (missing “to”)."},
      "A hedge can also come from **distancing**: “It has been suggested that…”, “According to Mensah (2020)…” (an invented example). These show that a claim comes from others and is not yet settled."
    ]},
    {h:"A scale of certainty", body:[
      "Hedges and boosters sit on a scale. This is a rough guide: the exact strength of each word depends on the sentence and the field.",
      {fig:'<svg viewBox="0 0 340 352"><text x="170" y="18" text-anchor="middle">More certain</text><rect class="d-rose" x="10" y="28" width="320" height="52" rx="6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="48">Certain (booster)</text><text class="s" x="20" y="68">X causes Y. It is clear that X causes Y.</text><rect class="d-gold" x="10" y="88" width="320" height="52" rx="6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="108">Very likely</text><text class="s" x="20" y="128">X almost certainly causes Y.</text><rect class="d-sage" x="10" y="148" width="320" height="52" rx="6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="168">Likely</text><text class="s" x="20" y="188">X probably causes Y. X is likely to cause Y.</text><rect class="d-lup" x="10" y="208" width="320" height="52" rx="6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="228">Possible</text><text class="s" x="20" y="248">X may (could) cause Y. It is possible that…</text><rect class="d-panel" x="10" y="268" width="320" height="52" rx="6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="20" y="288">Weaker possibility</text><text class="s" x="20" y="308">X might cause Y. One possibility is that…</text><text x="170" y="342" text-anchor="middle">Less certain</text></svg>', alt:"A vertical scale from more certain at the top to less certain at the bottom. Certain, a booster: X causes Y; it is clear that X causes Y. Very likely: X almost certainly causes Y. Likely: X probably causes Y; X is likely to cause Y. Possible: X may or could cause Y; it is possible that. Weaker possibility: X might cause Y; one possibility is that.", cap:"Choose the level the evidence supports. The boundaries are rough, not fixed rules."},
      "For the matching scale of reporting verbs (proves, shows, suggests, may contribute to), see {{academic-style}} in Research Skills."
    ]},
    {h:"Boosters: when to sound sure", body:[
      "Boosters include adverbs (**clearly, certainly, undoubtedly, definitely**), phrases (**in fact, indeed, it is clear that, there is no doubt that**) and strong reporting verbs (**demonstrate, establish, confirm, show**). Boosters are not wrong. They are right when:",
      {list:[
        "the evidence is strong and consistent (“The data clearly show a decline: all 12 monitoring sites recorded lower levels.”);",
        "you are stating something well established in your field;",
        "you want to emphasise a point you have just supported."
      ]},
      {warn:"Be careful with “obviously” and “of course”. They tell the reader that something needs no evidence, which can sound dismissive to someone who doesn’t find it obvious. And “prove” is rarely justified outside mathematics and logic: one study almost never proves a general claim."}
    ]},
    {h:"Where to hedge, and where not", body:[
      "Hedge the parts of a claim that go **beyond what you observed**:",
      {list:[
        "**explanations** of why something happened (“This may reflect…”);",
        "**generalisations** beyond your sample, place or time (“in similar towns”);",
        "**causal claims** from studies that only show an association (“was associated with” rather than “caused”);",
        "**predictions and recommendations** (“is likely to”, “could consider”)."
      ]},
      "Don’t hedge what you measured. “Attendance rose by 12% in the three schools” needs no “may”. Hedging a plain result makes readers wonder whether you trust your own data.",
      {table:[
        ["Problem","Example","Better"],
        ["Over-claiming","Our survey proves that walking improves sleep.","In our survey, adults who walked more reported better sleep, which suggests a link worth testing."],
        ["Over-hedging","It could possibly be suggested that scores might perhaps have risen.","Scores rose by 2 points."],
        ["Hedging the wrong part","Attendance may have risen by 12%. This proves the scheme works.","Attendance rose by 12%. This suggests that the scheme helped."]
      ]},
      {tip:"Expectations about directness differ between academic traditions and languages, and between fields. Following the conventions of the place you are writing for is a practical skill, not a judgement on your own tradition."}
    ]},
    {h:"Writing concisely", body:[
      "[[concision|Concision]] means using no more words than clarity needs. It is different from being short: cutting a definition, a hedge or a limitation makes a text shorter but less accurate. Edit for clarity first, length second.",
      {table:[
        ["Wordy","Concise"],
        ["due to the fact that","because"],
        ["in order to","to"],
        ["at this point in time","now"],
        ["in the event that","if"],
        ["has the ability to","can"],
        ["It is important to note that the sample was small.","The sample was small."],
        ["There are many farmers who use drip irrigation.","Many farmers use drip irrigation."]
      ]},
      "**Redundancy** means saying the same thing twice: “past history”, “final outcome”, “each and every”, “completely eliminate”, “a total of 25 people” (when nothing else is being added up). Keep one word.",
      {h:"Nominalisation overload"},
      "[[nominalisation|Nominalisation]] ({{noun-phrases-nominalisation|Unit 1}}) turns verbs and adjectives into nouns: reduce → reduction, analyse → analysis. It is useful: “This reduction…” sums up a whole earlier sentence in two words, which helps cohesion. But too many nominalisations in one sentence bury the action and the actor.",
      {table:[
        ["Overloaded","Clearer"],
        ["The implementation of a reduction in the use of plastic bags by supermarkets was achieved.","Supermarkets reduced their use of plastic bags."],
        ["The researchers conducted an analysis of the data.","The researchers analysed the data."],
        ["The committee made a decision to give consideration to the proposal.","The committee decided to consider the proposal."]
      ]},
      "A quick test: find the main action in the sentence. If it is hidden in a noun (reduction, analysis, decision), try making it the verb again, with the actor as the subject.",
      {h:"A final checklist for a paragraph"},
      {ol:[
        "Does the topic sentence state one controlling idea?",
        "Does every sentence support it?",
        "Does each sentence start from given information, and is every “this” clear?",
        "Is every transition signal accurate?",
        "Is each claim as strong as the evidence, with one hedge where needed and none on measured facts?",
        "Is the register formal enough for the genre?",
        "Are articles, agreement and tenses right?",
        "Can any empty words go without losing meaning?"
      ]},
      "Unit 3 builds this into a full {{editing-proofreading|editing and proofreading}} routine.",
      {terms:["booster","modal-verb","concision"]}
    ]}
   ],
   try:[
    {id:"e1u2-l5-x1", type:"sort", q:"Sort each word or phrase: does it usually hedge a claim (make it more cautious) or boost it (make it more certain)?", buckets:["Hedge","Booster"], items:[
      {t:"may", b:0, why:"A modal verb of possibility: “This may reflect…”."},
      {t:"appears to", b:0, why:"A hedging verb: the writer reports how things look, not certain fact."},
      {t:"it is likely that", b:0, why:"An it-clause that marks probability, not certainty."},
      {t:"approximately", b:0, why:"An approximator: it signals that a number is not exact."},
      {t:"clearly", b:1, why:"A booster adverb: it tells the reader the point is beyond doubt."},
      {t:"undoubtedly", b:1, why:"A strong booster: no doubt at all."},
      {t:"demonstrates", b:1, why:"A strong reporting verb: it claims the evidence is convincing."},
      {t:"in fact", b:1, why:"A booster phrase that insists on the truth of what follows."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l5-x2", type:"mcq", q:"Complete the sentence correctly: “Higher temperatures _____ the risk of crop failure.”", options:[
      {t:"may increase", ok:true, why:"A modal is followed by the base form of the verb: may + increase."},
      {t:"may increases", why:"After a modal, the verb never takes -s, even with a singular subject."},
      {t:"may to increase", why:"Modals are followed by the base form without “to”."},
      {t:"may increased", why:"After a modal, use the base form. For the past, use “may have increased”."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l5-x3", type:"mcq", q:"Dmitri surveyed 300 adults in one city. Those who walked more reported better sleep. Which sentence is best for his discussion section?", options:[
      {t:"In this sample, adults who walked more reported better sleep, which suggests a link worth testing in other settings.", ok:true, why:"It reports the finding accurately (reported sleep, in this sample), uses one hedge (“suggests”), and doesn’t claim cause or generalise beyond the city."},
      {t:"Walking improves sleep.", why:"A causal claim about everyone, from one survey in one city. A survey shows an association, not a cause, and the sample was one city."},
      {t:"It might possibly be that walking could perhaps be linked to sleep.", why:"Over-hedged: four hedges make Dmitri sound unsure of what he found. He did find an association; say so once, carefully."},
      {t:"Walking clearly causes better sleep in all adults.", why:"Two boosters (“clearly”, “all”) and a causal claim the design can’t support."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l5-x4", type:"spot", q:"Kenji is checking his discussion paragraph. Tap every claim that is **too strong** for the evidence and needs a hedge. Some parts are fine as they are.", ask:"over-claims", parts:[
      "In our study, ",{t:"average test scores rose by 8 points", flag:false, why:"A measured result. Report it plainly; no hedge needed."}," after the tutoring programme began. ",{t:"This proves that tutoring raises scores.", flag:true, why:"One study can’t prove cause, especially when scores also rose at schools without tutoring. “This suggests that tutoring may raise scores” fits the evidence."}," However, ",{t:"scores also rose at two comparison schools without tutoring", flag:false, why:"Another measured fact. Reporting it honestly is good practice."},", so other factors ",{t:"may have contributed", flag:false, why:"An appropriate hedge on an explanation."},". ",{t:"Tutoring will clearly work in every school in the country.", flag:true, why:"A booster (“clearly”), a confident prediction (“will”) and a huge generalisation (“every school”), from one study. A cautious version: “The programme could be tested in other schools.”"}," ",{t:"Further research could test the programme in rural schools.", flag:false, why:"A suitably cautious recommendation."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l5-x5", type:"mcq", q:"Lerato measured a 2-point rise in scores. Her draft says: “It could possibly be suggested that the results might perhaps indicate a small rise.” Which revision is best?", options:[
      {t:"Scores rose by 2 points.", ok:true, why:"It is a measured result, so it needs no hedge. Giving the number is more precise than “a small rise”."},
      {t:"It could be suggested that scores might have risen by 2 points.", why:"Still two hedges on a fact she measured. Hedging a plain result makes readers doubt the data."},
      {t:"Scores clearly rose dramatically.", why:"A booster and an exaggeration: 2 points is not dramatic, and “clearly” adds nothing."},
      {t:"Scores possibly rose.", why:"It hedges a measured fact and loses the size of the change."}
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l5-x6", type:"match", q:"Match each wordy phrase to a concise replacement with the same meaning.", pairs:[
      ["due to the fact that","because","Four words for one, with no extra meaning."],
      ["in order to","to","“In order” rarely adds anything before “to”."],
      ["at this point in time","now","A long phrase for a short word."],
      ["in the event that","if","Both introduce a condition."],
      ["has the ability to","can","“Can” already means ability or possibility."]
    ]},
    {id:"e1u2-l5-x7", type:"mcq", q:"Which is the clearest rewrite of: “The implementation of a reduction in the use of plastic bags by supermarkets was achieved.”?", options:[
      {t:"Supermarkets reduced their use of plastic bags.", ok:true, why:"The actor (supermarkets) becomes the subject and the hidden action (reduce) becomes the verb. Nothing important is lost."},
      {t:"A reduction in plastic bag use was implemented and achieved by supermarkets.", why:"Still built around nouns (reduction, use) and passive verbs, and “implemented and achieved” is redundant."},
      {t:"Supermarkets stopped using plastic bags.", why:"Shorter, but it changes the meaning: “reduced” is not “stopped”. Concision must keep the meaning."},
      {t:"There was a reduction of bags.", why:"It drops the actor (supermarkets) and the word “plastic”, and still hides the action in a noun."}
    ]}
   ],
   apply:{intro:"Use your own writing, or a draft you are working on now.", tasks:[
    {t:"Do a hedge and booster audit", how:["Take a discussion or conclusion section you have written (or 200 words of any argument you have written).","Highlight every hedge in one colour and every booster in another.","For each claim, ask: is this something I measured, or an interpretation, generalisation or prediction?","Remove hedges from measured facts, add one to any unhedged interpretation, and remove stacked hedges."], write:"Hedges I found… Boosters I found… Changes I made, and why…"},
    {t:"Borrow cautious phrases well", how:["Open the Academic Phrasebank page “Being cautious” (phrasebank.manchester.ac.uk).","Choose four phrases you would really use: one for explaining results, one for implications, one for recommendations and one for avoiding over-generalisation.","Write each into a sentence about your own topic, replacing the example content with yours."], write:"Explaining results… Implications… Recommendations… Avoiding over-generalisation…"},
    {t:"Cut 15% without losing meaning", how:["Choose a paragraph of about 150–200 words that you have written.","Count the words. Your target is about 15% fewer.","Cut empty phrases, redundant pairs and overloaded nominalisations. Keep every hedge, definition, limitation and citation that is needed.","Ask: is anything less clear or less accurate now? If so, put it back."], write:"Word count before… after… Three cuts I made… Anything I put back…"},
    {t:"Run the final checklist", how:["Take the 150-word paragraph you wrote in Lesson 4 (or write a new one).","Go through the eight questions in this lesson’s checklist, one at a time.","Fix one kind of problem at a time, rather than everything at once."], write:"Checklist results… What I fixed…"}
   ], proof:"A paragraph with its hedges and boosters audited, four cautious phrases rewritten for your own topic, a paragraph cut by about 15% with nothing important lost, and a checked paragraph."},
   read:[
     {book:"phrasebank", where:"“Being cautious”", note:"Phrases for distancing, explaining results cautiously, advising caution and avoiding over-generalisation."},
     {book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H3 “Clear and Effective Sentences”: “Conciseness” (“Use Action Verbs” and “Cut Unnecessary Words and Phrases”)", note:"More examples of wordy phrases and their replacements."}
   ]
  }
  ],
  checkpoint:["e1u2-q02","e1u2-q05","e1u2-q12","e1u2-q15","e1u2-q20","e1u2-q23","e1u2-q25","e1u2-q30","e1u2-q33","e1u2-q38","e1u2-q40","e1u2-q43"],
  codex:[
   {id:"formal-informal-choices", title:"Formal and informal choices in academic English", aka:["formality","contractions","phrasal verbs","vague language","general you","US and UK spelling"], tags:["academic english","writing","style"],
    summary:"Which everyday features to change in a formal essay (contractions, conversational phrasal verbs, the general “you”, vague words) and which are fine.",
    levels:{
     starter:[
      "Academic writing is usually **formal**. That does not mean long words. It means precise words, written rather than spoken style, and attention on the ideas.",
      "In a formal essay or report, most writers change four things:",
      {list:[
        "**Contractions** → full forms: “don’t” → “do not”, “it’s” → “it is”.",
        "**Conversational phrasal verbs** → a precise single verb: “look into” → “investigate”, “go up” → “increase”.",
        "**The general “you”** → a specific noun: “you” → “patients”, “voters”, “new residents”.",
        "**Vague words** → specific ones: “things”, “stuff”, “a lot of”, “really”, “get”."
      ]},
      "Many phrasal verbs are completely normal in research writing: carry out, set up, point out, focus on, rely on. Keep those.",
      {key:"Ask of every word: does it say exactly what I mean? That one question fixes most formality problems."}
     ],
     builder:[
      "**Contractions.** Avoid them in formal genres; keep possessive apostrophes (“the study’s aim”); write “cannot” as one word; keep contractions inside direct quotations.",
      "**Phrasal verbs.** Sort them into three groups: standard in academic writing (carry out, consist of, account for, result in); conversational, with a better single verb (find out → determine, come up with → devise, get rid of → eliminate, cut down on → reduce, put off → postpone, talk about → discuss); and borderline ones such as “look at”, which appear in published writing but have formal alternatives (examine, consider).",
      "When you swap in a single verb, **check its grammar** in a learner dictionary: “discuss” takes no “about”; “investigate” takes no “into” (but “an investigation into” is fine).",
      "**“You” and “I”.** Replace the general “you” with a specific noun or a different structure (“Smoking increases the risk of…”). “I” and “we” for what the authors did are accepted in many fields and recommended by some style guides, including APA; other fields prefer impersonal forms. Check local guidance.",
      "**Vague words.** Replace “a lot of” with a number, “many” (countable) or “much”, “considerable” or “a great deal of” (uncountable). Replace “thing” with the actual noun. Remove “really” and “very” or use a precise word.",
      "**Spelling.** US and UK spelling are both correct; choose one (or the one your institution or journal requires) and be consistent. Set your spell checker to match."
     ],
     pro:[
      "Formality is a matter of **genre and audience**, not a single scale. A reflective journal, a blog for the public and a grant application each have their own norms. Even within research articles, fields differ: some humanities writing is personal and lively; many science methods sections are impersonal. Read recent examples of the exact genre you are writing.",
      "Phrasal verbs are a good example of why simple rules mislead. Corpus-based grammars of English, such as the *Longman Grammar of Spoken and Written English* (Biber and colleagues, 1999), report phrasal verbs (verb + adverb particle) as much more frequent in conversation and fiction than in academic prose, but some of them are very common in academic writing because they have no exact single-word equivalent (carry out, set up). Prepositional verbs such as “depend on”, “consist of” and “focus on” are a separate group in that grammar, and many of them are everyday academic vocabulary. The useful skill is not avoiding a word class; it is noticing which items in your field’s writing are conversational.",
      "Single Latinate verbs are not automatically better. “Utilise” for “use”, “commence” for “start” and “ascertain” for “find out” often make writing stiffer without adding precision. Choose the word that is both precise and natural in your field.",
      "Starting a sentence with “And” or “But”, splitting an infinitive (“to carefully measure”) and ending a sentence with a preposition are not grammar errors in English. Some tutors and older style guides discourage them; follow your audience’s expectations, but don’t treat them as rules of the language.",
      "Spelling varieties extend beyond US and UK: Canadian, Australian, Indian, Nigerian, Singaporean and other Englishes have their own established conventions. Academic publishers usually specify US or UK spelling, and some accept either if it is consistent. Quotations keep their original spelling."
     ]},
    related:["academic-sentences","concise-writing","academic-style"],
    read:[{book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H5 “Words and Language”"}],
    sources:[{t:"OpenStax Writing Guide with Handbook: Handbook", url:"https://openstax.org/books/writing-guide/pages/handbook"}], checked:"2026-11"},

   {id:"academic-sentences", title:"Sentence patterns in academic English", aka:["clauses","simple compound complex sentences","subordinators","comma splice","run-on sentence","sentence fragment","parallel structure"], tags:["academic english","grammar","writing"],
    summary:"How clauses build simple, compound and complex sentences, how to join ideas, and how to fix comma splices, run-ons, fragments and faulty parallelism.",
    levels:{
     starter:[
      "Every sentence is built from **clauses**. A clause has a subject and a verb that shows tense (“Rainfall declined”).",
      "An **independent clause** can stand alone. A **dependent clause** begins with a word like because, although, when, if or which, and can’t stand alone.",
      {list:[
        "**Simple**: one independent clause. “Rainfall declined.”",
        "**Compound**: two independent clauses joined by and, but, so (and a comma) or a semicolon. “Rainfall declined, and yields fell.”",
        "**Complex**: an independent clause plus a dependent clause. “Because rainfall declined, yields fell.”"
      ]},
      "Three common errors: a **comma splice** (two sentences joined by only a comma), a **run-on** (two sentences with nothing between them) and a **fragment** (part of a sentence on its own, such as “Because rainfall declined.”).",
      {key:"“However” and “therefore” can’t join two sentences with a comma. Use a full stop or a semicolon before them."}
     ],
     builder:[
      "**Coordinators** (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) join equal clauses; put a comma before them when they join two independent clauses. **Subordinators** show dependence and a precise relationship: reason (because, since, as), contrast (although, while, whereas), condition (if, unless), time (when, once, until), purpose (so that). A dependent clause placed first takes a comma after it.",
      "**Conjunctive adverbs** (however, therefore, moreover, consequently, nevertheless, thus) are adverbs, not conjunctions. Correct patterns: “X. However, Y.” / “X; however, Y.” / “X. Y, however, …”.",
      "Fixes for a comma splice or run-on: a full stop; a semicolon; a comma plus a coordinator; or a subordinator that makes one clause dependent. Choose the one that shows the right relationship.",
      "Fixes for a fragment: attach it to the neighbouring sentence, or add the missing subject or verb. Watch sentences that begin with because, although, which, such as or an -ing form.",
      "Two learner traps: “Although…, but…” (use one joining word) and “Despite + clause” (use “despite + noun phrase” or “although + clause”).",
      "**Parallel structure**: items in a list or pair have the same form (“to plan, assess and give feedback”), including after both…and, either…or, not only…but also, and in bullet lists.",
      "**Sentence variety**: a paragraph of only short simple sentences hides the relationships between ideas; a paragraph of only long compound-complex sentences tires the reader. Use the structure that shows how ideas relate, and keep most sentences to one main idea."
     ],
     pro:[
      "Terminology varies between grammars. Some call subordinators “subordinating conjunctions”; some call conjunctive adverbs “linking adverbials” or “connectors”. Some handbooks (including the OpenStax handbook) separate comma splices from run-ons (fused sentences); others call both run-ons. The fixes are the same.",
      "Relative clauses (who, which, that) are another kind of dependent clause. A **defining** relative clause identifies which one (“the students who completed the survey”) and takes no commas. A **non-defining** clause adds extra information and is set off by commas (“The survey, which took ten minutes, …”). US style guides often prefer “that” for defining clauses; UK writers use “which” or “that”. Both are standard.",
      "Academic prose often carries information in **noun phrases** rather than clauses (“the rapid growth of informal settlements on the edges of large cities”). A sentence can be grammatically simple and still dense. When readers struggle, turning a long noun phrase back into a clause (“informal settlements grew rapidly on the edges of large cities”) often helps.",
      "Fragments are used deliberately in journalism, advertising and literature, and occasionally for emphasis in less formal academic genres. In formal essays and reports, treat them as errors.",
      "The serial (Oxford) comma before the last item in a list is required by some guides (APA among them) and omitted by others; either is correct if consistent. Use it where leaving it out could cause ambiguity."
     ]},
    related:["formal-informal-choices","grammar-trouble-spots","cohesion"],
    read:[{book:"owg", where:"Handbook, sections H3 “Clear and Effective Sentences” and H4 “Sentence Errors”"}],
    sources:[{t:"OpenStax Writing Guide with Handbook: Handbook", url:"https://openstax.org/books/writing-guide/pages/handbook"}], checked:"2026-11"},

   {id:"grammar-trouble-spots", title:"Grammar trouble spots: articles, nouns, agreement, tense and the passive", aka:["articles","a an the","zero article","countable and uncountable nouns","subject-verb agreement","tense in academic writing","passive voice"], tags:["academic english","grammar"],
    summary:"The grammar points that cause most problems in academic writing, with the usual patterns and the real exceptions.",
    levels:{
     starter:[
      "**Countable nouns** have a plural (study → studies). **Uncountable nouns** don’t (research, evidence, information, advice, equipment, feedback). Uncountable nouns take a singular verb and normally no “a/an”.",
      "**Articles.** A singular countable noun needs a word in front (a, the, this…). Use “the” when the reader knows which one; “a/an” for one of many; no article for plural or uncountable nouns used generally (“Water is essential”). “A” or “an” depends on the sound: an hour, a university.",
      "**Agreement.** Find the real subject: “The results of the survey show…” (results → show).",
      "**Tense.** Usually present for general facts and what your text does; past for your method and results; present perfect for research up to now. Fields vary.",
      "**Passive.** be + past participle (“was measured”). Useful when the actor doesn’t matter; risky when it hides who is responsible."
     ],
     builder:[
      "**Both countable and uncountable** with different meanings: experience, work, paper. **Data**: plural in APA and many sciences (“data are”), uncountable for many writers (“data is”); be consistent. **Research**: usually uncountable; use “studies” to count. Learn the Greek and Latin plurals: criteria, phenomena, analyses, hypotheses.",
      "**“The” checklist**: mentioned before; identified by a following phrase (“the effect of X”); unique or obvious (“the literature”); superlatives and ordinals (“the largest”, “the first”).",
      "**Agreement traps**: the number of (singular) vs a number of (plural); each/every/one of (singular); there is/are (agrees with the noun after); -ics subjects (economics is); collective nouns (singular in US English; singular or plural in UK English).",
      {table:[
        ["Section","Usual tense (APA guidance)"],
        ["Literature review","past or present perfect"],
        ["Method","past (or present perfect)"],
        ["Results","past"],
        ["Discussion of implications","present"],
        ["Conclusions, limitations, future work","present"]
      ]},
      "In the humanities, [[signal-phrase|signal phrases]] are usually in the present (“Okafor argues…”). Use the past simple with a finished time and the present perfect for time up to now (“since 2015, … has introduced”).",
      "**Passive**: verbs with no object (occur, happen, arise, exist, rise, die) have no passive. APA recommends the active in most cases and accepts the passive when the thing acted on matters more.",
      "**Common learner patterns to check in your own writing**: missing articles before singular countable nouns (“Survey showed…”), plural forms of uncountable nouns (“informations”, “equipments”), agreement with the wrong noun in long subjects, the present perfect with a finished time (“In 2020, we have collected…”) and passives of verbs that take no object (“was occurred”)."
     ],
     pro:[
      "Article choice depends on what writer and reader share, which is why simple rules leak. Generic reference can use three forms: “The smartphone has changed reading habits”, “Smartphones have changed…” and (less often) “A smartphone can…”. Fixed expressions follow their own patterns (“at home”, “by car”, “in practice”) and some differ between varieties (UK “in hospital”, “at university”; US “in the hospital”, “in college”). Proper names mostly take no article, except plural names and many names containing common nouns (the Philippines, the United Kingdom) and rivers and oceans.",
      "Agreement often follows meaning (notional number) rather than strict grammatical form. Some cases are fully standard in writing (“A number of studies have…”, “The majority of participants were…”); others, such as plural verbs after collective nouns, depend on the variety. “None of the samples was/were” is both used; traditional guides prefer singular, and modern usage accepts plural. When guides disagree, consistency and clarity matter more than the choice.",
      "Tense in reporting also carries **stance**: present (“Lee argues”) can present a view as current and alive in the debate; past (“Lee found”) ties a finding to a specific study. Writers sometimes switch tense to show which ideas they still treat as current.",
      "The passive interacts with information structure: it lets writers keep the topic in subject position and move long or new information to the end (given → new). It is also used for distancing (“It has been argued that…”). Critical discourse analysts note that agentless passives and nominalisations can obscure responsibility, for example in reports about job losses or evictions. In analytical writing, ask whether the actor matters, and name it when it does."
     ]},
    related:["academic-sentences","cohesion","hedging-and-boosting","dictionaries-corpora"],
    read:[{book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H7 “Verbs”"}],
    sources:[{t:"APA Style: Verb tense", url:"https://apastyle.apa.org/style-grammar-guidelines/grammar/verb-tense"},{t:"APA Style: Active and passive voice", url:"https://apastyle.apa.org/style-grammar-guidelines/grammar/active-passive-voice"},{t:"OpenStax Writing Guide with Handbook: Handbook", url:"https://openstax.org/books/writing-guide/pages/handbook"}], checked:"2026-11"},

   {id:"paragraph-structure", title:"Paragraph structure", aka:["topic sentence","supporting sentences","concluding sentence","controlling idea","paragraph unity"], tags:["academic english","writing","structure"],
    summary:"A topic sentence with a controlling idea, supporting sentences that keep to it, and a concluding or linking sentence.",
    levels:{
     starter:[
      "A paragraph develops **one main idea**. A common academic shape:",
      {ol:[
        "A **topic sentence** that states the main idea (usually first).",
        "**Supporting sentences**: explanation, evidence, examples and analysis.",
        "A **concluding or linking sentence** that sums up the point or connects to the next paragraph."
      ]},
      "A good topic sentence has a topic and a **controlling idea**: “Community health workers [topic] have improved access to basic care in many rural areas [controlling idea].”",
      "Avoid topic sentences that are too broad (“Health is important”), too narrow (a single fact) or just announcements (“This paragraph will discuss…”).",
      {key:"If you can’t say a paragraph’s point in one sentence, it is probably doing two jobs."},
      "A paragraph is like a room with one purpose. If you find a bed in the kitchen, move it to the bedroom: a sentence on a different idea belongs in a different paragraph."
     ],
     builder:[
      "**Unity**: every sentence supports the controlling idea. The common failure is **drift**, a true sentence that belongs in another paragraph. When you notice drift, move the sentence, cut it, or widen the topic sentence if the paragraph really is about both ideas.",
      "**Supporting sentences** can explain, give evidence (data, citations), give examples, compare, or answer an objection. Most strong paragraphs contain some **analysis**: sentences that say what the evidence means and how it supports the point.",
      "**Concluding sentences** should add something: the significance of the point (“This suggests…”), a qualification (“However, this applied only to…”), or a link forward. Repeating the topic sentence in other words wastes the reader’s time.",
      "Research Skills describes the same shape as four moves (topic sentence, evidence, analysis, link) in {{argument-structure}}. A quick structural check is to read only the first sentence of each paragraph: together they should outline your argument.",
      {table:[["Weak topic sentence","Problem","Stronger"],["Water is important.","Too broad","Village water committees kept hand pumps working longer when they controlled repair budgets."],["The district had 40 pumps in 2023.","Too narrow: one fact","Pump repairs were faster in districts with local repair budgets."],["This paragraph is about pumps.","An announcement","Local control of repair budgets was the main reason pumps stayed in use."]]},
      "Language for concluding sentences: “This suggests that…”, “These findings indicate…”, “Taken together, …”, “This raises the question of…”. Language for linking forward: “…which the next section examines”, “A second factor, however, is…”."
     ],
     pro:[
      "The topic-sentence-first paragraph is a strong convention in English academic writing, especially in essays and in the humanities and social sciences. It is not universal. Some paragraphs open with a transition sentence and state the point second; some build to the point; very short paragraphs may be used for emphasis or for a single step in a procedure. Methods sections often use paragraphs as containers for one procedure rather than one claim.",
      "Contrastive rhetoric research, beginning with Robert Kaplan’s 1966 article on cultural thought patterns, argued that paragraph organisation differs between languages. That early work was widely criticised as oversimplified and as treating English patterns as the norm. Later research, which Ulla Connor proposed calling **intercultural rhetoric** (2002), looks at genre, discipline and context, and at the smaller cultures of fields and classrooms, rather than national stereotypes. The practical point remains: readers of English academic texts generally expect the main point early.",
      "Paragraph length depends on genre, field and medium (web texts favour shorter paragraphs). Splitting is usually better than a page-long paragraph; but a string of two-sentence paragraphs often signals that ideas have not been developed or connected.",
      "For writers in an additional language, a practical routine is to write a one-line “point” for each paragraph before drafting, then check each finished paragraph against its line. This separates planning the argument from finding the words, so you have fewer things to think about at once while writing."
     ]},
    related:["cohesion","academic-sentences","argument-structure"],
    read:[{book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H2 “Paragraphs and Transitions”"}],
    sources:[{t:"OpenStax Writing Guide with Handbook: Handbook", url:"https://openstax.org/books/writing-guide/pages/handbook"}], checked:"2026-11"},

   {id:"cohesion", title:"Cohesion, coherence and transition signals", aka:["coherence","given-new","cohesive devices","transition words","linking words","reference"], tags:["academic english","writing","structure"],
    summary:"The language links between sentences (cohesion), the sense of the whole (coherence), given-to-new order, clear reference and transition signals with exact meanings.",
    levels:{
     starter:[
      "**Cohesion** is the language that links sentences: pronouns (it, they), “this” plus a noun, repeated key words and transition signals (however, therefore). **Coherence** is whether the ideas make sense together: one main point, a logical order and no gaps.",
      "**Given → new.** Start a sentence with something the reader already knows and end with something new. The new idea then starts the next sentence. “Many coastal towns depend on fishing. Fishing is threatened by warmer seas.”",
      "**Clear reference.** After “this” or “these”, add a noun: “This increase…”, “These findings…”.",
      "**Transition signals** each claim a relationship: however (contrast), therefore (result), moreover (another point in the same direction), for example (example).",
      {key:"A wrong transition signal is worse than none, because it tells the reader something false about your logic."}
     ],
     builder:[
      {table:[
        ["Device","Example"],
        ["Reference","The council raised fares. This increase…"],
        ["Repetition of key terms","…water use. Water use rose fastest in…"],
        ["Related word forms","Prices rose. The rise…"],
        ["Substitution and ellipsis","Some districts introduced fees; others did not."],
        ["Transition signals","However, … As a result, …"]
      ]},
      "Keep key terms consistent: switching between “participants”, “subjects” and “respondents” can suggest different groups.",
      "Exact meanings matter. **In contrast** compares two things; **on the contrary** rejects the previous statement. **On the other hand** introduces the other side of a question, not an extra point. **Nevertheless** concedes and continues. **For example** can start a sentence; **such as** introduces a noun phrase inside one.",
      "Punctuation: “However, …” at the start; “…; however, …” between clauses; “…, however, …” inside a clause.",
      "The passive and “this/these + noun” are two tools for keeping given information first.",
      "**Pronoun reference.** “It” and “they” refer most naturally to the nearest suitable noun, or to the subject of the previous sentence. If two nouns could fit, repeat the noun instead. “This” on its own can refer to a whole idea, which is often unclear; add a summary noun (this rise, this approach, these differences).",
      "**Choosing a signal**: first decide the relationship (addition, contrast, concession, result, example, restatement, summary), then pick a signal from that group. If you can’t name the relationship, the sentences may need reordering, or one of them may not belong."
     ],
     pro:[
      "M. A. K. Halliday and Ruqaiya Hasan’s *Cohesion in English* (1976) classified cohesive ties into five types: reference, substitution, ellipsis, conjunction and lexical cohesion. They treated cohesion as a property of the text itself. Later critics, such as Patricia Carrell in “Cohesion Is Not Coherence” (*TESOL Quarterly*, 1982), stressed that coherence also depends on the reader’s background knowledge and ability to build a sensible interpretation, so a text can be cohesive and still incoherent.",
      "The given-new principle comes from the study of **information structure** (in the Prague School tradition, often discussed as theme and rheme). Two common progressions in academic writing are the **linear** chain (the new information of one sentence becomes the theme of the next) and the **constant theme** (several sentences share the same starting point, as in a description of one method). Paragraphs that jump between unrelated themes feel disjointed even when each sentence is correct.",
      "Learner corpus studies have looked closely at connectors. Sylviane Granger and Stephanie Tyson (*World Englishes*, 1996), for example, compared essays by learners with essays by native-speaker students. They found no overall overuse of connectors, but clear **overuse of some individual connectors and underuse of others**. Other studies report learners misusing connectors whose meaning is narrower than it looks, such as “on the contrary”. A useful revision step is to delete each connector and ask whether the relationship is still clear; restore it only where it adds information.",
      "Singular “they” for a person of unknown or unspecified gender is endorsed by APA Style and accepted by many other guides. It helps avoid both awkward “he or she” constructions and inaccurate assumptions."
     ]},
    related:["paragraph-structure","academic-sentences","grammar-trouble-spots"],
    read:[{book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H2 “Paragraphs and Transitions”: “Transitions”"},{book:"phrasebank", where:"“Comparing and contrasting” and “Explaining causality”"}],
    sources:[{t:"Academic Phrasebank: Comparing and contrasting", url:"https://www.phrasebank.manchester.ac.uk/compare-and-contrast/"},{t:"Academic Phrasebank: Explaining causality", url:"https://www.phrasebank.manchester.ac.uk/explaining-cause-and-effect/"},{t:"OpenStax Writing Guide with Handbook: Handbook", url:"https://openstax.org/books/writing-guide/pages/handbook"}], checked:"2026-11"},

   {id:"hedging-and-boosting", title:"Hedging and boosting: the language in depth", aka:["hedges","boosters","modal verbs","cautious language","stance markers"], tags:["academic english","writing","stance"],
    summary:"The words and grammar for making claims more cautious or more certain, and where each belongs.",
    levels:{
     starter:[
      "A **hedge** makes a claim more cautious (may, might, could, appears to, probably, it is likely that, approximately). A **booster** makes it more certain (clearly, certainly, undoubtedly, demonstrate, in fact).",
      "Use them to match your words to your evidence. Hedge interpretations, explanations, generalisations and predictions. Don’t hedge what you measured: “Attendance rose by 12%” needs no “may”.",
      "Modal verbs are followed by the base verb: “may increase”, not “may increases” or “may to increase”.",
      "Avoid **stacking**: “might possibly suggest” has three hedges where one will do.",
      {key:"Hedging is precision about certainty, not weakness."},
      "A quick test: underline each verb that reports a finding or an idea (shows, suggests, proves, may…). Then ask of each: did I measure this, or am I interpreting, generalising or predicting? Measured facts need no hedge; the others usually need one."
     ],
     builder:[
      {table:[
        ["Kind","Examples"],
        ["Modal verbs","may, might, could, would; can (general possibility)"],
        ["Verbs","appear to, seem to, tend to, suggest, indicate"],
        ["Adverbs","possibly, probably, perhaps, generally, largely, partly"],
        ["It-clauses","it is likely / possible / unlikely that…"],
        ["Nouns","one possibility is that…, there is some evidence that…"],
        ["Approximators","approximately, around, in most cases, some"],
        ["Distancing","it has been suggested that…, according to…"]
      ]},
      "Grammar points: modal + have + past participle for past possibility (“may have caused”); modal + be + past participle for the passive (“could be explained”); “is likely to + verb” or “it is likely that + clause”. “Will likely rise” is common, especially in North American English; some UK editors prefer “will probably rise”.",
      "Boosters fit strong, consistent evidence and well-established knowledge. Use “obviously” and “of course” sparingly; they can sound dismissive. “Prove” rarely fits empirical research.",
      "The Academic Phrasebank’s “Being cautious” page lists phrases for distancing, explaining results, advising caution, implications, recommendations and avoiding over-generalisation.",
      "Where hedges cluster: discussion sections (explanations and implications), conclusions (recommendations and future research) and introductions (claims about previous work). Methods and results sections usually need far fewer.",
      "**Over-hedging** has two forms: stacking several hedges on one claim (“might possibly suggest”), and hedging facts you measured (“scores may have risen by 2 points”). Both make writers sound less sure than their evidence allows."
     ],
     pro:[
      "Ken Hyland’s research (*Hedging in Scientific Research Articles*, 1998; *Metadiscourse: Exploring Interaction in Writing*, 2005) treats hedges and boosters as part of **interpersonal** meaning: ways writers position themselves towards their claims and their readers. Hedges open space for other views and protect the writer; boosters close it down and show commitment. Expert writers balance both.",
      "Hyland and others have found that the use of hedges and boosters varies by **discipline**: writers in the humanities and social sciences tend to use more stance markers, and take more explicitly involved positions, than writers in the natural sciences and engineering (some later studies suggest the gap is changing over time), and the preferred devices differ (for example, more modal verbs in some fields, more reporting verbs in others). Comparing your drafts with published articles in your own field is the best guide.",
      "Hedges also differ in **what** they qualify. Some reduce the writer’s commitment to the truth of a proposition (“may”, “possibly”); others reduce the precision or scope of the claim (“approximately”, “in most cases”, “in this sample”). Scope hedges are often more honest and informative than vague modal hedging: “in three of the four regions” says more than “in some regions may”.",
      "Translation can shift stance. Words that look equivalent across languages may carry different strength, and academic traditions differ in how directly claims are made. For writers moving between languages, the useful question is not “which is correct?” but “what will this reader, in this field, take this word to mean?”"
     ]},
    related:["academic-style","reading-critically-language","concise-writing","grammar-trouble-spots"],
    read:[{book:"phrasebank", where:"“Being cautious”"}],
    sources:[{t:"Academic Phrasebank: Being cautious", url:"https://www.phrasebank.manchester.ac.uk/using-cautious-language/"}], checked:"2026-11"},

   {id:"concise-writing", title:"Writing concisely", aka:["concision","wordiness","empty phrases","redundancy","nominalisation overload"], tags:["academic english","writing","style"],
    summary:"Saying exactly what you mean with no wasted words, while keeping hedges, definitions and limitations.",
    levels:{
     starter:[
      "**Concise** writing uses no more words than clarity needs. Three kinds of waste are easy to spot:",
      {list:[
        "**Empty phrases**: due to the fact that → because; in order to → to; at this point in time → now; it is important to note that → (delete).",
        "**Redundancy**: past history, final outcome, each and every, completely eliminate.",
        "**Hidden verbs**: made a decision → decided; conducted an analysis of → analysed."
      ]},
      "Concise is not the same as short. Never cut a hedge, a definition, a limitation or a citation just to save words.",
      {key:"Clarity first, length second."},
      "A useful habit: after drafting, read each sentence and ask “Can I say this in fewer words without losing anything?” If yes, cut. If cutting removes a hedge, a definition, a number or a citation, put it back.",
      "Concise writing is kinder to readers, especially readers working in an additional language, because every word they decode carries meaning."
     ],
     builder:[
      "**“There is/are … who/that”** openers can usually go: “There are many farmers who use drip irrigation” → “Many farmers use drip irrigation.”",
      "**“It is … that”** openers often add nothing: “It is clear that the results are mixed” → “The results are mixed” (unless you want “clearly” as a booster).",
      "**Nominalisation overload.** Nominalisations (reduce → reduction) are useful for summarising and for cohesion (“This reduction…”). The problem is several in one sentence with a weak verb: “The implementation of a reduction in the use of plastic bags by supermarkets was achieved” → “Supermarkets reduced their use of plastic bags.” Find the main action, make it the verb, and put the actor first.",
      "**A method**: count the words in a paragraph; aim to cut 10–20%; then reread to check that nothing became less clear or less accurate.",
      "Watch meaning when you cut: “reduced” is not “stopped”; “some” is not “many”.",
      {table:[["Weak verb + noun","Action verb"],["make a decision","decide"],["conduct an analysis of","analyse"],["give consideration to","consider"],["reach a conclusion","conclude"],["have a discussion","discuss"]]},
      "**Redundant pairs and modifiers** are another easy cut: “basic fundamentals”, “future plans”, “joint collaboration”, “mutual cooperation”, “end result”, “absolutely essential”. One word of each pair already carries the meaning. Keep a list of the ones you use often, because most writers repeat the same few habits."
     ],
     pro:[
      "Many style guides and the OpenStax handbook recommend action verbs over noun phrases with weak verbs (reach a decision → decide; hold a meeting → meet). This also tends to make the actor visible, which matters in analytical and policy writing.",
      "Dense noun phrases are partly a feature of the genre: research writing packs information into nouns, and readers in a field are used to it. Concision for an expert audience is not the same as simplicity for a general one. Edit to the reader you have.",
      "Some apparent wordiness does real work. “A total of 25 participants” is redundant in mid-sentence, but some style guides prefer not to begin a sentence with a numeral, so writers may use it there. “In order to” can occasionally prevent ambiguity in a long sentence. Hedges and limitations are essential content.",
      "Word limits in assignments and journals make concision a practical necessity. A useful order of cuts: first remove repetition of ideas between paragraphs; then empty openers and redundancy; then over-hedging; only then consider cutting content.",
      "Concision also interacts with cohesion. Removing every repeated key term or every “this + noun” can make text shorter but harder to follow. The goal is economy of meaning, not the smallest possible word count."
     ]},
    related:["hedging-and-boosting","formal-informal-choices","noun-phrases-nominalisation","academic-style"],
    read:[{book:"owg", where:"Handbook, section H3 “Clear and Effective Sentences”: “Conciseness”"}],
    sources:[{t:"OpenStax Writing Guide with Handbook: Handbook", url:"https://openstax.org/books/writing-guide/pages/handbook"}], checked:"2026-11"}
  ],
  terms:[
   {id:"contraction", term:"Contraction", def:"A short form that joins two words with an apostrophe, such as “don’t” (do not) or “it’s” (it is). Usually avoided in formal academic writing.", codex:"formal-informal-choices"},
   {id:"phrasal-verb", term:"Phrasal verb", def:"A verb plus a small word such as “up”, “out” or “into” that together have one meaning, such as “carry out” or “come up with”. Grammars often call verb + preposition forms such as “rely on” prepositional verbs. Some are standard in academic writing; others are conversational.", codex:"formal-informal-choices"},
   {id:"clause", term:"Clause", def:"A group of words with a subject and a verb that shows tense, such as “rainfall declined”. Sentences are built from clauses.", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"independent-clause", term:"Independent clause", def:"A clause that can stand alone as a complete sentence: “The sample was small.”", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"dependent-clause", term:"Dependent clause", def:"A clause that can’t stand alone because it begins with a word such as because, although, when, if or which: “because the sample was small”.", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"compound-sentence", term:"Compound sentence", def:"A sentence with two or more independent clauses, joined by a coordinator (and, but, so…) or a semicolon.", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"complex-sentence", term:"Complex sentence", def:"A sentence with one independent clause and at least one dependent clause: “Because rainfall declined, yields fell.”", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"coordinating-conjunction", term:"Coordinating conjunction", def:"One of the seven words for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so, which join words, phrases or clauses of equal status.", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"subordinator", term:"Subordinator", def:"A word such as because, although, whereas, if, unless or when that begins a dependent clause and shows how it relates to the main clause. Also called a subordinating conjunction.", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"conjunctive-adverb", term:"Conjunctive adverb", def:"A linking adverb such as however, therefore, moreover or consequently. It shows a relationship between ideas but can’t join two clauses with only a comma.", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"comma-splice", term:"Comma splice", def:"The error of joining two independent clauses with only a comma: “Prices rose, many families moved away.”", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"run-on-sentence", term:"Run-on sentence", def:"Two or more independent clauses run together with no punctuation or joining word between them (also called a fused sentence). Some guides also use the term for comma splices.", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"sentence-fragment", term:"Sentence fragment", def:"Part of a sentence punctuated as if it were complete, such as a dependent clause on its own (“Because fares were lower.”). Usually an error in formal writing.", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"parallel-structure", term:"Parallel structure", def:"Using the same grammatical form for items that do the same job in a sentence, such as all verbs or all nouns in a list.", codex:"academic-sentences"},
   {id:"countable-noun", term:"Countable noun", def:"A noun with a singular and a plural that can take “a” or “an” (a study, two studies). Uncountable nouns, such as research, evidence and information, have no plural and take a singular verb.", codex:"grammar-trouble-spots"},
   {id:"article-grammar", term:"Articles", def:"The words “a”, “an” and “the”, plus the “zero article” (no article). They show whether the reader knows which thing you mean, or whether you mean things in general.", codex:"grammar-trouble-spots"},
   {id:"subject-verb-agreement", term:"Subject–verb agreement", def:"Matching the verb to its subject: a singular subject takes a singular verb (the study shows) and a plural subject a plural verb (the studies show).", codex:"grammar-trouble-spots"},
   {id:"tense-in-academic-writing", term:"Tense in academic writing", def:"The conventions for choosing tenses in each part of an academic text, such as past for your method and results and present for general facts and implications. They vary by field and style guide.", codex:"grammar-trouble-spots"},
   {id:"passive-voice", term:"Passive voice", def:"A verb form (be + past participle) that makes the thing acted on the subject: “The samples were measured.” The actor can be left out or added with “by”.", codex:"grammar-trouble-spots"},
   {id:"supporting-sentence", term:"Supporting sentence", def:"A sentence in a paragraph that explains, gives evidence or examples for, or analyses the main idea stated in the topic sentence.", codex:"paragraph-structure"},
   {id:"concluding-sentence", term:"Concluding sentence", def:"The last sentence of a paragraph, which sums up the point, says why it matters or links to the next paragraph.", codex:"paragraph-structure"},
   {id:"cohesion", term:"Cohesion", def:"The language links that connect sentences in a text, such as pronouns, repeated key terms and transition signals.", codex:"cohesion"},
   {id:"coherence", term:"Coherence", def:"Whether the ideas in a text make sense together for the reader: a clear main point, a logical order and no gaps.", codex:"cohesion"},
   {id:"cohesive-device", term:"Cohesive device", def:"Any word or structure that links sentences: a pronoun, “this” plus a noun, a repeated key term, a related word form or a transition signal.", codex:"cohesion"},
   {id:"transition-signal", term:"Transition signal", def:"A word or phrase such as however, therefore, moreover or for example that tells the reader exactly how the next idea relates to the previous one.", codex:"cohesion"},
   {id:"given-new", term:"Given-to-new order", def:"Starting a sentence with information the reader already knows and ending with new information, which can then start the next sentence.", codex:"cohesion"},
   {id:"booster", term:"Booster", def:"A word or phrase that makes a claim more certain or emphatic, such as clearly, certainly, undoubtedly or demonstrates. The opposite of a hedge.", codex:"hedging-and-boosting"},
   {id:"modal-verb", term:"Modal verb", def:"A helping verb such as can, could, may, might, will, would, should or must, followed by the base form of a verb (“may increase”). Often used to hedge.", codex:"hedging-and-boosting"},
   {id:"concision", term:"Concision", def:"Saying exactly what you mean in no more words than clarity needs, without cutting needed hedges, definitions or limitations.", codex:"concise-writing"}
  ],
  bank:[
   /* ---- Lesson 1 ---- */
   {id:"e1u2-q01", lesson:"e1u2-l1", level:1, q:"In academic English, what does “formal” mainly mean?", options:[
     {t:"Precise, written rather than spoken in style, and focused on the ideas", ok:true, why:"These are the core features. Formality is about saying exactly what you mean for a reader who can’t ask questions."},
     {t:"Using the longest and rarest words available", why:"Long words are not more formal or precise. “Use” is usually better than “utilise”."},
     {t:"Writing every verb in the passive", why:"The passive is useful in some places, but formal writing uses active verbs too."},
     {t:"Never expressing any opinion", why:"Academic writing makes and argues claims. Formality is about how you express them."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q02", lesson:"e1u2-l1", level:2, q:"Which phrasal verb is standard in research writing and does not need replacing?", options:[
     {t:"carry out (a survey)", ok:true, why:"“Carry out” is a normal, widely used phrasal verb in academic writing."},
     {t:"come up with (a theory)", why:"Conversational. “Propose”, “develop” or “devise” is more precise in a formal essay."},
     {t:"get rid of (errors)", why:"Conversational. “Eliminate” or “remove” is the formal choice."},
     {t:"cut down on (costs)", why:"Conversational. “Reduce” is shorter and more formal."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q03", lesson:"e1u2-l1", level:1, q:"Which sentence uses an apostrophe that is fine in a formal report?", options:[
     {t:"The study’s main aim was to compare two clinics.", ok:true, why:"This apostrophe shows possession, not a contraction. It is completely formal."},
     {t:"The results weren’t clear.", why:"“Weren’t” is a contraction. Write “were not”."},
     {t:"It’s been difficult to recruit participants.", why:"“It’s” is a contraction of “it has”. Write “It has been…”."},
     {t:"The team couldn’t finish the analysis.", why:"“Couldn’t” is a contraction. Write “could not”."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q04", lesson:"e1u2-l1", level:2, q:"Sione writes: “When you lose your job, you often feel anxious.” Which revision is best for a formal essay?", options:[
     {t:"People who lose their jobs often experience anxiety.", ok:true, why:"It replaces the general “you” with a specific noun and keeps the meaning."},
     {t:"When one loses one’s job, one often feels anxious.", why:"Correct, but “one” repeated three times sounds stiff and old-fashioned. A specific noun reads better."},
     {t:"When you lose your job, you’re often anxious.", why:"Still uses the general “you”, and adds a contraction."},
     {t:"Losing jobs is kind of stressful.", why:"“Kind of” is vague and conversational, and the meaning has changed."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q05", lesson:"e1u2-l1", level:2, q:"Which sentence is correct?", options:[
     {t:"The authors discuss three possible causes.", ok:true, why:"“Discuss” takes a direct object with no preposition."},
     {t:"The authors discuss about three possible causes.", why:"“Discuss” does not take “about”. This error often comes from replacing “talk about”."},
     {t:"The authors investigate into three possible causes.", why:"“Investigate” takes a direct object. (“An investigation into” is fine; the verb takes no “into”.)"},
     {t:"The authors discusses three possible causes.", why:"“The authors” is plural, so the verb is “discuss”."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q06", lesson:"e1u2-l1", level:1, q:"What is the best advice about US and UK spelling in an academic essay?", options:[
     {t:"Either is correct; choose one (or the one required) and use it consistently.", ok:true, why:"Both are standard. Consistency is what readers and editors look for."},
     {t:"Always use US spelling, because it is the international standard.", why:"There is no single international standard; many publishers use UK spelling."},
     {t:"Always use UK spelling, because it is more academic.", why:"UK spelling is not more academic than US spelling."},
     {t:"Mix them freely; nobody notices.", why:"Inconsistent spelling is noticeable and looks careless."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q07", lesson:"e1u2-l1", level:3, q:"Amara’s draft says: “Lots of things affect how stuff gets to market.” What is the main weakness?", options:[
     {t:"It is vague: the reader can’t tell which factors, which goods or how many.", ok:true, why:"“Lots of”, “things”, “stuff” and “gets” all hide the specific meaning. The fix requires deciding what she means, for example “Road quality and fuel prices affect how quickly farmers can transport crops to market.”"},
     {t:"It is too short to be a sentence.", why:"Length is not the problem; it is a complete sentence."},
     {t:"It uses the passive voice.", why:"There is no passive in the sentence."},
     {t:"It should start with “And”.", why:"Starting with “And” would not help, and the problem is vagueness."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q08", lesson:"e1u2-l1", level:3, q:"Kenji’s tutor says, “Use fewer phrasal verbs.” Kenji replaces every one, including “carried out”, with long Latin-based verbs such as “utilise” and “effectuate”. What is the problem?", options:[
     {t:"He has made the writing stiffer without making it more precise; some phrasal verbs are standard, and simple words like “use” are often best.", ok:true, why:"The aim is precision. Change conversational phrasal verbs; keep standard ones and prefer natural words."},
     {t:"There is no problem: Latin-based verbs are always more academic.", why:"Rare Latinate verbs can make writing heavy and less clear. They are not automatically more academic."},
     {t:"Phrasal verbs are forbidden, so he should delete the sentences.", why:"Phrasal verbs are not forbidden; many are standard."},
     {t:"He should use contractions instead.", why:"Contractions are less formal, not more."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q09", lesson:"e1u2-l1", level:2, q:"Which sentence is correct in a formal report?", options:[
     {t:"The committee published its report in May.", ok:true, why:"“Its” (no apostrophe) means “belonging to it”. Possessive pronouns such as its, their and whose never take an apostrophe."},
     {t:"The committee published it’s report in May.", why:"“It’s” always means “it is” or “it has”, and “published it is report” makes no sense. The possessive is “its”."},
     {t:"The committee published its’ report in May.", why:"There is no form “its’” in English. The possessive is simply “its”."},
     {t:"The committee’s report was published in it’s final form in May.", why:"“The committee’s” is a correct possessive, but “it’s final form” should be “its final form”: “it’s” means “it is”."}
   ]},
   /* ---- Lesson 2 ---- */
   {id:"e1u2-q10", lesson:"e1u2-l2", level:1, q:"What is a dependent clause?", options:[
     {t:"A clause that can’t stand alone, often beginning with because, although, when or which", ok:true, why:"It has a subject and verb but depends on an independent clause to complete the sentence."},
     {t:"A clause with no verb", why:"Every clause has a verb that shows tense. A group of words with no verb is a phrase."},
     {t:"Any clause that comes second in a sentence", why:"Position doesn’t decide it. A dependent clause can come first or second."},
     {t:"A short sentence", why:"Short sentences are usually independent clauses."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q11", lesson:"e1u2-l2", level:1, q:"What type of sentence is this? “Although the clinic was small, it served three villages.”", options:[
     {t:"Complex", ok:true, why:"One dependent clause (“Although the clinic was small”) plus one independent clause."},
     {t:"Simple", why:"It has two clauses, so it is not simple."},
     {t:"Compound", why:"A compound sentence joins two independent clauses. “Although…” is dependent."},
     {t:"A fragment", why:"It has an independent clause (“it served three villages”), so it is complete."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q12", lesson:"e1u2-l2", level:2, q:"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?", options:[
     {t:"Costs rose; therefore, the project was delayed.", ok:true, why:"A semicolon joins the two independent clauses, and “therefore” takes a comma after it."},
     {t:"Costs rose, therefore the project was delayed.", why:"A comma splice: “therefore” is an adverb and can’t join clauses with only a comma."},
     {t:"Costs rose therefore the project was delayed.", why:"A run-on: no punctuation between the two clauses."},
     {t:"Costs rose, therefore, the project was delayed.", why:"Still a comma splice: commas alone can’t join two independent clauses."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q13", lesson:"e1u2-l2", level:2, q:"Which of these is a sentence fragment?", options:[
     {t:"Such as maize, cassava and millet.", ok:true, why:"“Such as” introduces examples inside a sentence; on its own this has no subject or verb."},
     {t:"Farmers grew maize, cassava and millet.", why:"Complete: subject “farmers”, verb “grew”."},
     {t:"Maize was the main crop.", why:"Complete: subject “maize”, verb “was”."},
     {t:"Most farmers grew several crops, such as maize and millet.", why:"Complete: “such as” is correctly inside a full sentence."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q14", lesson:"e1u2-l2", level:2, q:"Which sentence is correct?", options:[
     {t:"Although prices fell, demand stayed low.", ok:true, why:"One joining word (“although”) for one contrast."},
     {t:"Although prices fell, but demand stayed low.", why:"“Although” and “but” both mark the contrast; English uses only one."},
     {t:"Despite prices fell, demand stayed low.", why:"“Despite” needs a noun phrase: “Despite the fall in prices”."},
     {t:"Prices fell, however demand stayed low.", why:"A comma splice with “however”. Use a semicolon or full stop."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q15", lesson:"e1u2-l2", level:3, q:"Fatima writes: “The training aimed to improve confidence, reducing errors and better teamwork.” What should she change?", options:[
     {t:"Make the list parallel: “to improve confidence, reduce errors and strengthen teamwork.”", ok:true, why:"The original mixes a verb (improve), an -ing form (reducing) and a noun phrase (better teamwork). Parallel items share one form."},
     {t:"Add “and” before every item.", why:"That doesn’t fix the mixed forms."},
     {t:"Split it into three fragments.", why:"Fragments would create new errors."},
     {t:"Nothing: lists can mix any forms.", why:"Mixed forms make lists harder to read; academic style expects parallel structure."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q16", lesson:"e1u2-l2", level:3, q:"Oluwaseun writes: “Since the bridge closed, traders have used the ferry.” A reader isn’t sure whether “since” means time (from that moment) or reason (because). What is the best fix if he means reason?", options:[
     {t:"Use “Because the bridge closed, traders now use the ferry.”", ok:true, why:"“Because” has only the reason meaning, so the ambiguity disappears."},
     {t:"Use “While the bridge closed, traders have used the ferry.”", why:"“While” means time or contrast, not reason, and the sentence no longer makes sense."},
     {t:"Use “Since the bridge closed traders have used the ferry.”", why:"Removing the comma doesn’t change the meaning of “since”."},
     {t:"Use “As the bridge closed, traders now use the ferry.”", why:"“As” can also mean time or reason, so it is still ambiguous."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q17", lesson:"e1u2-l2", level:2, q:"Which word is a coordinating conjunction that can join two independent clauses with a comma before it?", options:[
     {t:"yet", ok:true, why:"“Yet” is one of the seven coordinators (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so)."},
     {t:"however", why:"“However” is a conjunctive adverb; it needs a semicolon or full stop before it."},
     {t:"moreover", why:"“Moreover” is a conjunctive adverb, not a coordinator."},
     {t:"therefore", why:"“Therefore” is a conjunctive adverb, not a coordinator."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q18", lesson:"e1u2-l2", level:2, q:"Which sentence is correct?", options:[
     {t:"Because of the flood, the clinic closed for a week.", ok:true, why:"“Because of” is followed by a noun phrase (“the flood”). That is its normal pattern, like “despite”."},
     {t:"Because of the river flooded, the clinic closed for a week.", why:"“Because of” can’t be followed by a clause with a subject and verb. Write “Because the river flooded” or “Because of the flood”."},
     {t:"Because the flood, the clinic closed for a week.", why:"“Because” on its own needs a clause: “Because the river flooded”. Before a noun phrase, use “because of”."},
     {t:"Because the river flooded, so the clinic closed for a week.", why:"“Because” and “so” both mark the cause and result. English uses one joining word: drop “so”."}
   ]},
   /* ---- Lesson 3 ---- */
   {id:"e1u2-q19", lesson:"e1u2-l3", level:1, q:"Which noun is usually uncountable in academic English?", options:[
     {t:"information", ok:true, why:"No “an information” or “informations”; use “a piece of information” or “details”."},
     {t:"study", why:"Countable: a study, two studies."},
     {t:"finding", why:"Countable: a finding, several findings."},
     {t:"hypothesis", why:"Countable: one hypothesis, two hypotheses."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q20", lesson:"e1u2-l3", level:2, q:"Which sentence follows the usual pattern in modern academic English?", options:[
     {t:"There is little research on this question.", ok:true, why:"“Research” is usually uncountable, so it takes “little” and a singular verb."},
     {t:"There are few researches on this question.", why:"The plural “researches” exists but is rare in modern academic English, and editors usually change it. Use “few studies”."},
     {t:"There are many research on this question.", why:"“Many” needs a countable plural. Use “much research” or “many studies”."},
     {t:"There is a research on this question.", why:"“Research” doesn’t normally take “a”. Use “a study”."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q21", lesson:"e1u2-l3", level:1, q:"Which is correct?", options:[
     {t:"an hour-long interview", ok:true, why:"“Hour” begins with a vowel sound (the h is silent), so “an”."},
     {t:"an university course", why:"“University” begins with a “y” sound, so “a university”."},
     {t:"a MRI scan", why:"“MRI” begins with the sound “em”, so “an MRI scan”."},
     {t:"an one-year project", why:"“One” begins with a “w” sound, so “a one-year project”."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q22", lesson:"e1u2-l3", level:2, q:"Choose the correct sentence.", options:[
     {t:"The number of complaints has doubled.", ok:true, why:"“The number” is one figure, so it takes a singular verb."},
     {t:"The number of complaints have doubled.", why:"The subject is “the number”, which is singular."},
     {t:"A number of complaints has been received.", why:"“A number of” means “several”, so it takes a plural verb: “have been received”."},
     {t:"The numbers of complaint has doubled.", why:"“Complaint” should be plural after “number of”, and the phrase should be “the number”."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q23", lesson:"e1u2-l3", level:3, q:"Ana writes in her results section: “Scores rise by 10% in the second term.” Her tutor marks the verb. Why?", options:[
     {t:"Results of a completed study are usually reported in the past simple: “Scores rose…”.", ok:true, why:"Her results describe what happened in her study, so most fields expect the past."},
     {t:"Results must always be in the future tense.", why:"The future fits plans in a proposal, not results already found."},
     {t:"“Rise” can only be used in the passive.", why:"“Rise” has no object, so it can’t be passive at all."},
     {t:"Results must be in the present perfect.", why:"APA guidance and common practice use the past for results."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q24", lesson:"e1u2-l3", level:2, q:"Which sentence is correct?", options:[
     {t:"Since 2018, the city has built four new clinics.", ok:true, why:"“Since 2018” describes a period up to now, so the present perfect is needed."},
     {t:"Since 2018, the city built four new clinics.", why:"With “since” and a period up to now, use the present perfect, not the past simple."},
     {t:"In 2018, the city has built four new clinics.", why:"“In 2018” is a finished time, so use the past simple: “built”."},
     {t:"Since 2018, the city is building four new clinics.", why:"The present progressive doesn’t fit “since 2018”; use “has built” or “has been building”."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q25", lesson:"e1u2-l3", level:3, q:"A company report says: “Unfortunately, 300 jobs were lost this year.” Why might a critical reader object to the passive here?", options:[
     {t:"It hides who made the decision: the company cut the jobs.", ok:true, why:"The agentless passive makes the loss sound like something that just happened. Naming the actor shows responsibility: “The company cut 300 jobs.”"},
     {t:"The passive is always grammatically wrong.", why:"The passive is correct English and often useful."},
     {t:"“Lost” cannot be used in the passive.", why:"“Lose” takes an object, so it has a normal passive."},
     {t:"The sentence is in the present tense.", why:"“Were lost” is past."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q26", lesson:"e1u2-l3", level:2, q:"Which sentence is correct?", options:[
     {t:"A serious problem arose during the pilot study.", ok:true, why:"“Arise” has no object, so it is used only in the active."},
     {t:"A serious problem was arisen during the pilot study.", why:"“Arise” can’t be passive."},
     {t:"A serious problem was occurred during the pilot study.", why:"“Occur” can’t be passive."},
     {t:"A serious problem was happened during the pilot study.", why:"“Happen” can’t be passive."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q27", lesson:"e1u2-l3", level:3, q:"Kenji’s department asks students to follow APA Style. Which sentence matches APA’s usage?", options:[
     {t:"The data were collected in three provinces.", ok:true, why:"APA Style treats “data” as plural, so it takes a plural verb (“were”)."},
     {t:"The data was collected in three provinces.", why:"Many writers treat “data” as uncountable, and outside APA this is common and acceptable. But APA treats it as plural, and Kenji’s department follows APA."},
     {t:"The datas were collected in three provinces.", why:"“Data” is already a plural (the rare singular is “datum”), so there is no “datas”."},
     {t:"A data was collected in three provinces.", why:"“Data” doesn’t take “a” in either usage. For one item, write “a data point” or “one observation”."}
   ]},
   /* ---- Lesson 4 ---- */
   {id:"e1u2-q28", lesson:"e1u2-l4", level:1, q:"What are the two parts of a good topic sentence?", options:[
     {t:"A topic and a controlling idea", ok:true, why:"The topic says what the paragraph is about; the controlling idea says what you claim about it."},
     {t:"A question and an answer", why:"Some paragraphs open with a question, but that is not the standard definition."},
     {t:"A citation and a statistic", why:"Evidence usually belongs in the supporting sentences."},
     {t:"A greeting and a summary", why:"Paragraphs don’t have greetings."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q29", lesson:"e1u2-l4", level:1, q:"What is the difference between cohesion and coherence?", options:[
     {t:"Cohesion is the language links between sentences; coherence is whether the ideas make sense together.", ok:true, why:"You can point to cohesion in the words; coherence is about the logic and order of ideas."},
     {t:"They mean exactly the same.", why:"They are related but different. A text can be cohesive yet incoherent."},
     {t:"Cohesion is about spelling; coherence is about grammar.", why:"Neither term is about spelling or grammar accuracy."},
     {t:"Cohesion is for speech; coherence is for writing.", why:"Both apply to speech and writing."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q30", lesson:"e1u2-l4", level:2, q:"Choose the best signal: “The new seeds produced higher yields. _____, they cost twice as much as local varieties.”", options:[
     {t:"However", ok:true, why:"The higher price is a drawback that pulls against the benefit, so a contrast signal fits."},
     {t:"Therefore", why:"“Therefore” claims that the price follows logically from the yields. The writer is setting a drawback against a benefit, not drawing a conclusion."},
     {t:"For example", why:"Needing more water is not an example of higher yields."},
     {t:"In other words", why:"The second sentence does not restate the first."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q31", lesson:"e1u2-l4", level:2, q:"Which next sentence best follows given-to-new order? “Many schools now use tablets in class.”", options:[
     {t:"These tablets are often bought with short-term grants.", ok:true, why:"It starts with the given idea (“these tablets”) and adds new information at the end."},
     {t:"Short-term grants are often how tablets are bought by these schools.", why:"It starts with new information (short-term grants) and buries the link to the previous sentence."},
     {t:"Grants, short-term ones, are what buy them, often.", why:"Awkward and starts with new information; “them” is also unclear."},
     {t:"Short-term grants exist.", why:"It doesn’t connect to the previous sentence at all."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q32", lesson:"e1u2-l4", level:2, q:"“The policy did not reduce costs. _____, costs rose by 15%.” Which signal fits?", options:[
     {t:"On the contrary", ok:true, why:"It rejects the idea that costs fell and gives the opposite fact."},
     {t:"In contrast", why:"“In contrast” compares two different things; here the writer is correcting an expectation about one thing."},
     {t:"Moreover", why:"“Moreover” adds a point in the same direction; the second sentence intensifies the denial rather than adding a separate point."},
     {t:"For instance", why:"Rising costs are not an example of “did not reduce”; they go further."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q33", lesson:"e1u2-l4", level:3, q:"Priya’s paragraph uses “participants”, “subjects”, “respondents” and “people” for the same group. Her reader is confused. Why?", options:[
     {t:"In academic writing, different terms can suggest different groups; a key term should stay the same.", ok:true, why:"Consistent key terms are a cohesive device. Swapping synonyms for variety makes readers wonder if the groups differ."},
     {t:"Synonyms are grammatically incorrect.", why:"They are correct English; the problem is clarity."},
     {t:"She should have used pronouns only.", why:"Only pronouns would create unclear reference."},
     {t:"Academic writing never repeats words.", why:"Repetition of key terms is normal and helpful in academic writing."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q34", lesson:"e1u2-l4", level:3, q:"Tomás’s paragraph is about why a recycling scheme succeeded. One sentence says the town also has a new football stadium. What is the problem?", options:[
     {t:"The sentence breaks the paragraph’s unity: it doesn’t support the controlling idea.", ok:true, why:"It may be true, but it is off topic. Cut it or move it to a relevant paragraph."},
     {t:"The sentence is too short.", why:"Length is not the problem."},
     {t:"The sentence needs a transition signal.", why:"A signal can’t make an unrelated idea relevant."},
     {t:"The paragraph needs a second topic sentence.", why:"That would make the paragraph do two jobs."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q35", lesson:"e1u2-l4", level:2, q:"What should a concluding or linking sentence usually do?", options:[
     {t:"Say why the point matters, qualify it, or link to the next paragraph", ok:true, why:"It should add something, not just repeat."},
     {t:"Repeat the topic sentence in different words", why:"Pure repetition wastes the reader’s time."},
     {t:"Introduce a completely new topic", why:"A new topic needs its own paragraph."},
     {t:"Give the paragraph’s first piece of evidence", why:"Evidence belongs in the supporting sentences."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q36", lesson:"e1u2-l4", level:2, q:"“Water samples were collected from 12 wells.” Which next sentence keeps the given information (the samples) at the start?", options:[
     {t:"The samples were then tested for bacteria in a district laboratory.", ok:true, why:"It starts with the given idea (“the samples”) and ends with the new information (the test and where it happened). The passive makes this order possible."},
     {t:"A district laboratory then tested the samples for bacteria.", why:"Correct English, but it starts with new information (a laboratory the reader hasn’t met yet), so it doesn’t keep the samples first."},
     {t:"Bacteria were then tested for in the samples by a district laboratory.", why:"It starts with “bacteria”, which is new, and “tested for in” is awkward."},
     {t:"Testing then took place.", why:"It loses the link to the samples altogether and gives the reader almost no new information."}
   ]},
   /* ---- Lesson 5 ---- */
   {id:"e1u2-q37", lesson:"e1u2-l5", level:1, q:"Which word is a booster rather than a hedge?", options:[
     {t:"undoubtedly", ok:true, why:"It makes the claim more certain."},
     {t:"perhaps", why:"A hedge: it adds caution."},
     {t:"tend to", why:"A hedge: it softens a generalisation."},
     {t:"approximately", why:"A hedge (an approximator): it marks a number as not exact."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q38", lesson:"e1u2-l5", level:2, q:"Which sentence is grammatically correct?", options:[
     {t:"The drought may have reduced yields.", ok:true, why:"Modal + have + past participle expresses possibility in the past."},
     {t:"The drought may reduced yields.", why:"After a modal, use the base form, or “have + past participle” for the past."},
     {t:"The drought may to have reduced yields.", why:"Modals are never followed by “to”."},
     {t:"The drought mays have reduced yields.", why:"Modals never take -s."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q39", lesson:"e1u2-l5", level:2, q:"Which sentence is correct?", options:[
     {t:"Rents are likely to rise next year.", ok:true, why:"“Be likely to + base verb” is the standard pattern."},
     {t:"Rents are likely rise next year.", why:"“Likely” in this pattern needs “to”: “are likely to rise”."},
     {t:"Rents are likely that they rise next year.", why:"The it-pattern is “It is likely that rents will rise”."},
     {t:"Rents likely to rising next year.", why:"Missing verb “are”, and “to” needs the base form “rise”."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q40", lesson:"e1u2-l5", level:3, q:"Hana measured a fall in water use from 120 to 95 litres per day. Which sentence is best?", options:[
     {t:"Water use fell from 120 to 95 litres per day.", ok:true, why:"A measured result needs no hedge, and the figures are precise."},
     {t:"Water use may have possibly fallen somewhat.", why:"Two hedges on a measured fact, and the figures are lost."},
     {t:"Water use undoubtedly collapsed.", why:"A booster and an exaggeration: a fall of about a fifth is not a collapse."},
     {t:"It could be suggested that water use might have fallen.", why:"Over-hedged: she measured the fall."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q41", lesson:"e1u2-l5", level:3, q:"Mei’s study found that districts with more clinics had lower infant mortality. Which claim fits the evidence?", options:[
     {t:"More clinics were associated with lower infant mortality, possibly because care was easier to reach.", ok:true, why:"It reports an association and hedges the explanation, without claiming proven cause."},
     {t:"This proves that building clinics reduces infant mortality.", why:"An association across districts can’t prove cause; richer districts might have both more clinics and lower mortality."},
     {t:"Clinics clearly cause lower infant mortality everywhere.", why:"A causal claim, a booster and a generalisation beyond the data."},
     {t:"It might perhaps possibly be that clinics could be related.", why:"Over-hedged: she did find an association and can say so."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q42", lesson:"e1u2-l5", level:1, q:"Which is the most concise version that keeps the meaning of “in order to”?", options:[
     {t:"to", ok:true, why:"“In order” rarely adds meaning before “to”."},
     {t:"so as to", why:"Correct, and it keeps the meaning, but it uses three words where one will do."},
     {t:"for the purpose of", why:"Longer, not shorter, and it needs an -ing form after it (“for the purpose of reducing…”)."},
     {t:"because", why:"“Because” gives a reason, not a purpose, so the meaning changes."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q43", lesson:"e1u2-l5", level:3, q:"Dmitri cuts this sentence to save words: “Results suggest that the scheme may have helped in two of the three towns.” → “The scheme helped.” What went wrong?", options:[
     {t:"He cut the hedge and the limitation, so the claim is now stronger and wider than the evidence.", ok:true, why:"Concision must keep needed hedges and limits. A good cut: “The scheme may have helped in two of the three towns.”"},
     {t:"Nothing: shorter is always better.", why:"Concise is not the same as short. Accuracy comes first."},
     {t:"He should have added “clearly”.", why:"A booster would make the over-claim worse."},
     {t:"The new sentence is a fragment.", why:"“The scheme helped” is a complete sentence; the problem is accuracy."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q44", lesson:"e1u2-l5", level:2, q:"Which rewrite fixes nominalisation overload in “The committee made a decision to give consideration to the proposal”?", options:[
     {t:"The committee decided to consider the proposal.", ok:true, why:"The hidden actions (decision, consideration) become verbs, and nothing is lost."},
     {t:"A decision was made by the committee regarding consideration of the proposal.", why:"Still built on nouns, and now passive as well."},
     {t:"The committee approved the proposal.", why:"Shorter, but it changes the meaning: deciding to consider is not approving."},
     {t:"Consideration of the proposal was decided.", why:"It hides the actor and keeps the nouns."}
   ]},
   {id:"e1u2-q45", lesson:"e1u2-l5", level:2, q:"When is a booster such as “clearly” most appropriate in academic writing?", options:[
     {t:"When the evidence is strong and consistent, for example when all 12 monitoring sites recorded the same decline", ok:true, why:"Boosters fit claims that the evidence firmly supports. Used there, they show justified confidence."},
     {t:"When you expect a reader to disagree, to make the claim sound stronger", why:"A reader who disagrees needs evidence, not a stronger word. Boosting a disputed claim without support makes the writing less convincing."},
     {t:"In most sentences of a discussion section, so the writer sounds confident", why:"Boosters lose their force when used everywhere, and many discussion claims are interpretations that need a hedge instead."},
     {t:"When the evidence is weak, to make up for it", why:"That is over-claiming: the words promise more than the evidence can support."}
   ]}
  ]
});
